window.TTCS_DOCS = [{"id":"intro","pdf":"intro.pdf","kind":"info","title":"Course introduction","blurb":"Organization, grading, objectives and references.","topics":["grading","objectives","references"],"ar":1.3333,"pages":[{"t":"Basic Tools and Techniques in Computer Science","x":"Basic Tools and Techniques in Computer Science Vlad Olaru Bucharest University Department of Computer Science Email: vlad.olaru@fmi.unibuc.ro"},{"t":"Organization","x":"Organization Grading Laboratory 30 points weeks 1–7 only labs 2 – 7 are graded presence is mandatory cannot make up for lost labs Project 35 points weeks 8–14 2–3 student teams same points for each team member picked up from a list work done in the lab starting week 8 Veriﬁcation 35 points Passing grade: at least 50 points out of ‖ Organization Grading Laboratory 30 points weeks 1–7 only labs 2–7 are graded presence is mandatory cannot make up for lost labs Project 35 points weeks 8–14 2–3 student teams same points for each team member picked up from a list work done in the lab starting week 8 Veriﬁcation 35 points Passing grade: at least 50 points out of"},{"t":"Objectives","x":"Objectives getting used to command line work automatization operating systems diversity basics of networking system services"},{"t":"References","x":"References Operating systems Linux (open source) Unix-like systems (e.g., MacOS) Virtual machines VirtualBox (open source) QEMU (open source) KVM (open source) Theoretical reference A. Silberschatz, P.B. Galvin, and G. Gagne (2014). Operating system concepts essentials. John Wiley & Sons, Inc. http://codex.cs.yale.edu/avi/os-book/OS10/"},{"t":"Dinosaur book","x":"Dinosaur book"}]},{"id":"c1","pdf":"course1.pdf","kind":"lecture","n":1,"title":"Computer systems and the operating system","blurb":"What a computer system is made of, what an OS is and does, and how one is built and installed.","topics":["system structure","what is an OS","kernel","OS services","building Linux"],"ar":1.7778,"pages":[{"t":"Tools and Techniques in Computer Science","x":"Tools and Techniques in Computer Science Fall Semester 2026 Vlad Olaru"},{"t":"Course 1 - Outline","x":"Course 1 - Outline • computer system structure • what is an operating system • operating system services • system startup (the boot process)"},{"t":"Computer System Structure","x":"Computer System Structure • computer system components: Hardware – basic computing resources CPU, memory, input/output (I/O) devices Operating system controls and coordinates the use of hardware among programs and users Application programs – define how system resources are used to solve users' problems Word processors, compilers, web browsers, database systems, video games Users people, machines, other computers"},{"t":"Abstract View of a Computer's Components","x":"Abstract View of a Computer's Components"},{"t":"Computer System Organization","x":"Computer System Organization • computer system operation one or more processors and device controllers communicate through a common bus that provides access to memory end effect: concurrent execution of processors and devices competing for memory access cycles"},{"t":"How a Modern Computer Works","x":"How a Modern Computer Works"},{"t":"What Is an Operating System?","x":"What Is an Operating System? • a program that acts as an intermediary between the user and the computer hardware • operating system objectives: execute user programs and make it easier to solve problems make the computer system convenient to use in particular, responsible for defining software abstractions e.g., we work with files rather than disk blocks, and with network connections rather than bit strings handled by network adapters use the computer hardware efficiently"},{"t":"What Does an Operating System Do?","x":"What Does an Operating System Do? • depends on the point of view • users want ease of use and performance they do not care about resource utilization • however, large computers (eg, supercomputers) must meet the expectations of all users operating system = resource allocator and control program makes hardware use more efficient and manages the execution of user programs • workstation users have dedicated resources, but often use resources shared by servers • mobile devices (smartphones, tablets) have limited resources and are optimized for usability and battery life special user interfaces, touchscreens, speech recognition • some computers have limited interfaces or none at all, e.g., embedded systems in industrial equipment or automobiles they primarily run without user intervention"},{"t":"Definition of an Operating System","x":"Definition of an Operating System • there is no generally accepted definition • “the software shipped with the computer by the manufacturer” is a good approximation however, it varies greatly • “the program that runs continuously on the computer” is the so-called kernel of the operating system • the remainder is either a system program (supplied with the operating system, but not part of the kernel), or an application, meaning any program not associated with the operating system • current general-purpose operating systems (GPOS) and mobile operating systems also include middleware – a set of software frameworks that provide additional services to application developers, such as databases, multimedia, and graphics"},{"t":"Operating System Services","x":"Operating System Services • the operating system provides an execution environment for programs and services for programs and users • some operating system services provide user assistance functions: user interface – almost all operating systems have a user interface (UI) varies: Command-Line Interface (CLI), Graphical User Interface (GUI), touchscreen, Batch program execution – the operating system must be able to load a program into memory and execute it, and terminate it either normally or abnormally with an error indication input/output (I/O) operations – an executing program may request I/O, which may involve access to files or I/O devices file manipulation – the file system is of particular interest to users (programs need to read/write files and directories, create and delete them, search them, display information about them, and manage their access permissions)"},{"t":"Operating System Services (cont.)","x":"Operating System Services (cont.) • some operating system services provide user assistance functions (cont.): communication - processes may exchange information on the same computer or between networked computers communication may take place through shared memory or message passing error detection - the operating system must be constantly aware of possible errors they may occur in the CPU or memory, in I/O devices, or in user programs for each type of error, the operating system must take the appropriate action to ensure correct and consistent computation debugging facilities can substantially improve the ability of users and programmers to use the computer system efficiently"},{"t":"Operating System Services (cont.)","x":"Operating System Services (cont.) • some other operating system services ensure efficient system operation when resources are shared resource allocation – when multiple users or programs run concurrently, each requires resources resource types – CPU cycles, main memory, file storage, I/O devices execution logging – required to account for users' resource utilization and the types of resources used protection and security – information stored in multi-user or networked systems may have restricted access, and concurrent processes must not interfere with one another protection involves ensuring that all access to system resources is controlled system security against external users requires user authentication and extends to protecting external I/O devices from invalid access attempts"},{"t":"A View of Operating System Services","x":"A View of Operating System Services"},{"t":"Operating System Installation and Booting","x":"Operating System Installation and Booting • operating systems are generally designed to run on a class of systems with a variety of peripheral devices • typically, the operating system is already installed on the purchased computer however, other operating systems can be compiled and installed if an operating system is built from scratch write the operating system code configure it for the computer system on which it will run compile the operating system install the operating system boot the computer under the control of the new operating system"},{"t":"Example: Linux","x":"Example: Linux • download the Linux source code (http://www.kernel.org) • configure the kernel using “make menuconfig” • compile the kernel using “make” produce vmlinuz, the kernel image compile the kernel modules using “make modules” install the kernel modules into vmlinuz using “make modules” install the new kernel on the system using “make install”"}]},{"id":"c2","pdf":"course2.pdf","kind":"lecture","n":2,"title":"Boot, login, the shell and files","blurb":"From power-on to a prompt: boot loaders, init and runlevels, login, UID/GID, files, mounts, descriptors and redirection.","topics":["boot process","runlevels","login","UID / GID","file system","file descriptors","redirection"],"ar":1.7778,"pages":[{"t":"Tools and Techniques in Computer Science","x":"Tools and Techniques in Computer Science Fall Semester 2026 Vlad Olaru"},{"t":"Outline","x":"Outline • System startup (boot process) • User login process • User interface • Files and directories"},{"t":"Booting the system","x":"Booting the system • when the computer starts, the execution begins in a fixed place in the memory • the OS must be made available to HW in order to be able to turn it on a small piece of code – bootstrap loader, BIOS, stored in ROM or EEPROM locates the kernel, loads it into memory and starts it sometimes it's a two-step process, using a boot block located at a fixed address in the ROM code, which then loads the boostrap loader from the disk modern systems replace BIOS with Unified Extensible Firmware Interface (UEFI) • a common bootstrap loader is GRUB, it allows kernel selection from multiple disks, with different versions and options • the kernel program loads and then the system runs • boot loaders frequently allow different boot states, such as single user mode"},{"t":"Unix Boot Process","x":"Unix Boot Process • the first sector of the boot disk (MBR, or its successor GPT) disk partition table bootstrap code (boot loader)"},{"t":"Unix Boot Process (cont.)","x":"Unix Boot Process (cont.) • the loader identifies the boot partition and loads the kernel code (the kernel of the operating system) • Note: at this level there is no notion (abstraction) of file, but only disk sectors => two possible solutions 1. The loader knows a map of the disk sectors that contain the kernel code hardcoded solution, involves map updates when the kernel image on disk changes, disk defragmentation, etc 2. The loader has access to drivers that understand the structure of the file system on disk and can thus identify the kernel as a random file (using the file path) • eg, Linux boot loaders: Lilo, Grub"},{"t":"Unix Boot Process (cont.)","x":"Unix Boot Process (cont.) • the kernel image file (eg, /boot/vmlinuz for Linux) is loaded into memory and the kernel takes control of the HW machine • subsequently, the kernel executes: HW component initialization sequence instantiates the main components: process control, memory management, file management, accounting, system time management, HW protection and security mechanisms, etc remains resident in memory while waiting for external events (\"interrupt-driven program\") at the end of the initialization sequence executes the first process (ID = 1): /sbin/init init sets the operating mode (runlevel) defines the state of the compute machine after boot traditionally defined by a number between 0 and 6 historically (Unix systems), init looks for the runlevel in /etc/inittab then, calls the system service initialization scripts cf. the selected runlevel /etc/rc0.d/, /etc/rc1.d, …, /etc/rc6.d, /etc/rcS.d"},{"t":"Runlevels","x":"Runlevels • assigned to the operating mode of the machine 0 , power-off 1, single-user mode 2, multi-user without network 3, multi-user with network but without graphical interface 4, generally undefined, reserved for special uses 5, multi-user with network and graphical interface 6, reboot • Linux: init is called systemd, and runlevels are defined as targets, handled with specific commands (systemctl) • commands that handle runlevels: $ runlevel # prints the current runlevel, akin to “who –r” $ telinit <runlevel> # switches the kernel to the specified runlevel $ telinit 6 # reboots"},{"t":"Systems with or without GUI","x":"Systems with or without GUI • init is also responsible for starting the login processes for the user: depending on the runlevel: /sbin/getty respectively the desktop manager of X Window graphics interfaces (xdm, gdm, etc) runlevel 3: init starts getty on a preset number of terminals runlevel 5: init porneste getty + desktop manager historically (Unix systems), init searches in /etc/inittab for terminal association – login program (getty or xdm/gdm) • system switching between runlevels w/ or w/o graphical interface $ telinit 3 # disables GUI $ telinit 5 # enables back GUI Note: key combinations (like Ctrl-Alt-F1 in Linux) allow switching between terminals and GUI (usually, Ctrl-Alt-F7) in runlevel 5, but the GUI is not disabled!"},{"t":"User login to systems without a GUI","x":"User login to systems without a GUI • getty shows the login prompt • the user enters the username • getty calls /bin/login which sets up a new work session • login displays the password prompt searches /etc/passwd for an entry corresponding to the username checks the password (usually stored encrypted in another file, e.g. /etc/shadow) for the correct password, executes the shell associated with the identified entry associate environment variables with the shell some important variables (USER, SHELL, HOME) initialized with the values in the fields read from the corresponding entry in /etc/passwd • the shell displays a specific prompt (eg, $) and waits for the user's commands • init monitors the user's work session at the end of the task (shell exit), restart an instance of the getty program on that terminal"},{"t":"The shell","x":"The shell • Command Line Interpreter (CLI), allows direct entry of commands system program that takes user commands and executes them usable both interactively and batch (using shell scripts) executes both internal commands (executed within the interpreter) and external commands (programs loaded from the disk) main functionalities providing a working environment for the user (see env command) file and directory handling commands program execution control commands control and monitoring of I/O activities system administration (reserved for a special user commonly known as root, with UID = 0, see the first entry in /etc/passwd) etc. • eg, command interpreters: Bourne Shell (/bin/sh), Bourne Again Shell (bin/bash), C Shell (/bin/csh), Korn Shell (/bin/ksh), etc. /etc/shells contains the command interpreters available on the system"},{"t":"Bourne Shell (CLI)","x":"Bourne Shell (CLI)"},{"t":"Graphical interface - GUI","x":"Graphical interface - GUI • user-friendly interface usually composed of mouse, keyboard, and monitor icons represent files, programs, actions, etc clicking mouse buttons on objects in the interface determines various actions (providing information, options, executing functions, opening directories, etc.) invented at Xerox PARC • many systems today include both CLI and GUI Microsoft Windows has both GUI and CLI shell Apple Mac OS X has GUI with UNIX kernel UNIX and shells available Unix and Linux have CLIs (shells) with optional GUI (CDE, KDE, GNOME))"},{"t":"Mac OS X GUI","x":"Mac OS X GUI"},{"t":"User identification","x":"User identification • at login, the user receives an own ID (non-negative integer value by which the user is identified in the OS), the user ID (UID) obtained from the corresponding user input in /etc/passwd unique  assigned by root, the only one who has permission to write to /etc/passwd cannot be changed by the user used by the kernel to check whether user processes have the right to perform certain operations UID = 0 reserved for root or superuser (system administrator) • root processes have superuser privileges and usually bypass the checks that the kernel does for a series of operations • some of the kernel functions can only be executed by root processes • the root has total control over the computing system N.B.: for this reason, the initiative of system users who know the root password to run ordinary programs as root (UID = 0) is strongly discouraged"},{"t":"User identification (cont.)","x":"User identification (cont.) • at login the user also receives a GID (Group ID), also set by root in the corresponding entry in /etc/passwd allows sharing of resources between members of the same group, even if they have different UIDs in contrast, users with different GIDs can't access these shared group resources e.g.: directory entries for each file in the system contain the pair (UID,GID) of the owner of that file the ls -l shell command allows the file owner's IDs to be displayed • /etc/group assign readable names to user GIDs modifiable only by root lists also supplementary GIDs, i.e. the same user can have multiple GIDs (can belong to more than one group) • /usr/bin/id prints UID/GID $ id # UID/GID for the shell user $ id root # UID/GID for another user (root)"},{"t":"Files and directories","x":"Files and directories • file: OS-level abstraction for permanent data storage hides the details of the actual data storage on the disk easy-to-understand data structure model (e.g., Unix byte stream) grouped into directories referred to by name (can contain any character except '/’) attributes: type, size, owner, permissions, last modified time, etc eg Unix, the usual command for displaying attributes $ ls –l <filename> • directory/folder: file collection may contain other directories (subdirectories) way of organizing information, usually in a hierarchical manner"},{"t":"Files and directories (cont.)","x":"Files and directories (cont.) • Unix-like systems use a hierarchical directory structure starts from a special directory called root, designated by the character \"/\" • special directories created automatically when a new directory is created . current directory (newly created directory) .. parent directory (the directory in which a new entry corresponding to the newly created directory has been inserted) in the case of the root directory ./ and .. / represent the same directory, namely \"/\" • path: the sequence of filenames separated by the / absolute paths: always start with / relative paths: do not start with /, being interpreted relative to the current working directory • at login, the current working directory is set to the value obtained from /etc/passwd for the logged-in user (called home directory) • command to print entries to a directory: /bin/ls Note: an executable program is also represented by a path in the file system"},{"t":"File system","x":"File system • the special component of the OS that manages files and directories • structure data on disk in a specific format • provides the user with a uniform data access interface eg Unix, tree hierarchy of directories, with a common root • modern OSs are capable of integrating file systems with different formats into the same directory hierarchy VFS – Virtual Filesystem Switch (ext3, ext4, ntfs, vfat, etc) • become available to the user as a result of the mount operation $ mount –t ext4 /dev/sda1 / • the directory where the formatted disk is installed is called mountpoint • /etc/fstab: system-wide table with mountpoints inspected at OS boot time at boot time, the mountpoints in the table are installed as if “mount –a” has been called"},{"t":"Mountpoints","x":"Mountpoints"},{"t":"File descriptors","x":"File descriptors • non-negative integers used to identify files opened in the system allocated by the kernel when opening/creating a file by program subsequently used by the program for reading/writing the file • special descriptors when starting any program, the shell opens three special file descriptors for it: 0 standard input(stdin) 1 standard output (stdout) 2 standard error (stderr) usually associated with the login terminal (or the work terminal, in a graphical environment with multiple X terminals) /usr/bin/tty displays the terminal associated with a shell (generally not just the login terminal)"},{"t":"Redirection of I/O operations","x":"Redirection of I/O operations • redirecting I/O operations can be done programmatically or directly from the shell • the shell understands syntactic constructs of the following type as redirects of I/O operations [n] < filename redirects the readings from the descriptor n to the designated file; if n is missing, stdin is used << marker redirects stdin to the keyboard using an end-of-file marker (otherwise it's Ctrl-d); used for inserting ad-hoc documents [n] > filename redirects the writes on descriptor n to the designated file; if n is missing, stdout is used [n] >> filename adds the writes to descriptor n at the end of the designated file (\"append\"); if n is missing, stdout is used"},{"t":"Redirection of I/O operations","x":"Redirection of I/O operations • ex: $ echo “stdout redirected to out file” > out $ echo “add another line at the end of the out file” >> out $ cat < out $ cat << EOF >> out > add another line at the end of the OUT file > EOF $"}]},{"id":"c3","pdf":"course3.pdf","kind":"lecture","n":3,"title":"The command interpreter, files and permissions","blurb":"The bash environment, pipelines, job control, history, file types, set-UID, permissions, umask and links.","topics":["environment","bash config files","pipelines & lists","job control","history","file types","permissions","links"],"ar":1.7778,"pages":[{"t":"Tools and Techniques in Computer Science","x":"Tools and Techniques in Computer Science Fall Semester 2026 Vlad Olaru"},{"t":"Lecture 3 - outline","x":"Lecture 3 - outline • the command interpreter • files and directories (revisited)"},{"t":"The command interpreter (recap)","x":"The command interpreter (recap) • interactive mode (command-response) or batch mode (automating work with scripts) • in interactive mode, it displays a prompt indicates that a command (internal/external) is expected from the user usually $ or % for regular users, # for root defined by the value of the PS1 environment variable $ echo $PS1 continuation prompt in the PS2 environment variable, usually > $ cat << EOF >> out > we append one more line at the end of the file out > EOF $"},{"t":"The shell environment","x":"The shell environment • a list of pairs of the form name = value • name is the name of an internal shell variable Note: not every internal shell variable is an environment variable • the values of environment variables influence the behavior of the shell and of the external commands it launches e.g.: shell (PS1/2, PATH), gcc (CPATH, LIBRARY_PATH), ld (LD_LIBRARY_PATH), etc. set by the OS or by the user • names written in uppercase: PS1, SHELL, HOME, PATH, etc. • value dereferenced using the $ symbol, e.g. $SHELL = /bin/bash"},{"t":"Environment variables","x":"Environment variables • set with the built-in command export marks the variable as an environment variable $ export PS1=“my-new-prompt> ” • displayed with the command /usr/bin/env • in a C program, accessible through the third parameter of the main function of a program launched by the shell int main(int argc, char* argv[], char *envp[])"},{"t":"The Bourne-Again SHell","x":"The Bourne-Again SHell • /bin/bash, the successor of the first historical shell, /bin/sh (Bourne Shell) • configuration files start-up files read only at login system-wide: /etc/profile local, in the home directory: ~/.profile, ~/.bash_profile, ~/.bash_login their content is executed automatically at login start-up files executed whenever a terminal is created (rc file, run commands file) ~/.bashrc their content can be executed on demand with the command source (or “.”) source .bashrc . .bashrc logout file: ~/.bash_logout content executed when exiting the shell (with exit or Ctrl-d) Note: in Unix, Ctrl-d is the EOF character; typing Ctrl-d at the prompt terminates the shell • command history recorded in ~/.bash_history"},{"t":"Structure of bash commands","x":"Structure of bash commands • pipelines $ cmd1 | cmd2 | … |cmdn # parallel execution of commands $ cmd1 |& cmd2 |& … |& cmdn # “|&” is equivalent to “2>&1 |” • command lists $ cmd1; cmd2; …; cmdn # sequential execution of commands $ cmd1 && cmd2 && … && cmdn # conditional execution of commands $ cmd1 || cmd2 …. || cmdn • the bash variable “?” holds the termination code (exit status) of the last executed command (zero means success) $ echo $? Note: ? is not an environment variable!"},{"t":"Job control","x":"Job control • two categories of programs executed in the foreground, with R/W access to the terminal executed in the background • a command terminated by “&” runs in the background the shell immediately returns the prompt to the user $ cmd & • the execution of a foreground command can be suspended with ^Z (Ctrl-z) in fact, it is the SIGTSTP signal (kill –SIGTSTP <pid>) the command’s execution can later be resumed, either in the foreground or in the background • the jobs command lists the processes (jobs) currently run by the shell jobs are identified by number the job number can be used together with the following commands $ kill %n # terminates the process/job no. n $ fg %n # moves to the foreground the process no. n $ bg %n # moves job n to the background $ %n & # moves job n to the background"},{"t":"Command history control","x":"Command history control • ~/.bash_history • examples !n re-executes command no. n !-n re-executes command no. current – n !string re-executes the most recent command starting with string !?string? re-executes the most recent command containing string ^str1^str2 repeats the previous command, replacing str1 with str2 • interactively: Ctrl-r followed by a substring of the command searched for in the history"},{"t":"Internal commands","x":"Internal commands • built-in: executed directly by bash (bash functions) $ cd <dir> $ alias l=‘ls –l’ $ fg/bg/kill <job#> $ exit <status> # terminates the shell with exit code status $ exec <cmd> # replaces the bash image with the image of the new # process # e.g.: $ exec firefox"},{"t":"External commands","x":"External commands • programs on disk launched by the shell $ pwd $ echo string e.g. escape chars: echo –e \\a # bell echo –e “aaa\\tbbb” # horizontal tab echo –e “aaa\\v\\bbbb” # vertical tab + backspace echo –e “aaa\\t\\rbbb” # carriage return echo –e “aaa\\t\\nbbb” # newline"},{"t":"File types","x":"File types • ordinary files (regular files): contain data (text or binary) • directories: contain the names of other files and information about them can be read by processes that have the appropriate permissions ONLY the kernel can write to them! • special files, device type character: for character devices (e.g.: tty, serial port) block: for block-oriented devices (e.g.: disks) R/W operations are performed not through the FS but through the drivers Note: every device in the system is either a block file or a character file • FIFO: named pipe, IPC mechanism (Inter-Process Communication) | so-called anonymous pipes, link processes that are related connect unrelated processes files on disk, with a name and a FIFO access policy bidirectional, unlike |"},{"t":"File types (cont.)","x":"File types (cont.) • socket: abstraction for IPC (Inter Process Communication) over a network local communication channel (Unix socket, a kind of FIFO) communication channel between networked machines (TCP/IP socket) • symbolic link: a file that refers to another file in practice, the destination file (the symbolic link) contains the name of the source file (the referenced file) $ ln –s <source-file> <symbolic-link> • the long format of the ls command marks the file type in the first character: -, d, c, b, p, s, l • a general command that also distinguishes types of regular files (text, executables, images, etc.): $ file <file-name> ‖ File types (cont.) • socket: abstraction for IPC (Inter Process Communication) over a network local communication channel (Unix socket, a kind of FIFO) communication channel between networked machines (TCP/IP socket) • symbolic link: a file that refers to another file in practice, the destination file (the symbolic link) contains the name of the source file (the referenced file) $ ln –s <source-file> <symbolic-link> • the long format of the ls command marks the file type in the first character: -, d, c, b, p, s, l•a general command that also distinguishes types of regular files (text, executables, images, etc.): $ file <file-name>"},{"t":"Set UID, set GID","x":"Set UID, set GID • each process (program in execution) has associated with it real UID, GID: the real identity of the user, taken from /etc/passwd effective UID, GID saved set-UID, set-GID (copies of the effective UID/GID) • normally, real UID/GID = effective UID/GID • when a program is executed, a flag can be set in the attributes of the executable file such that: “for the duration of this program’s execution, the effective UID/GID of the process becomes the UID/GID of the owner of the program file” E.g.: the command for changing the user password $ passwd /usr/bin/passwd is a set-UID program owned by root, in order to have write permission on /etc/shadow"},{"t":"File access permissions","x":"File access permissions • file attributes • grouped into three categories user permissions: S_IRUSR, S_IWUSR, S_IXUSR group permissions: S_IRGRP, S_IWGRP, S_IXGRP permissions for other users: S_IROTH S_IWOTH, S_IXOTH special permissions: set-uid, set-gid, sticky bit • can be modified from the shell using the command chmod e.g.: chmod u+rw <file>, chmod g-x <file>, chmod o-rwx <file>, etc. or in octal chmod 755 <file>, chmod 644 <file> , etc. • access to a file: determined by the combination of the effective UID (or effective GID) of the executed command and the permission bits"},{"t":"umask","x":"umask • every newly created file has a default permission mask set with the umask command (a shell built-in) $ umask 022 • the bits set in umask are off in the permissions of the newly created file • usually, the mask is set in the shell configuration files (e.g., /etc/profile)"},{"t":"Deleting files","x":"Deleting files • a file can have several links to the same internal kernel structure (i-node) different names of the same file so-called hard links created with the command ln $ ln <source-file> <destination-file> deleting a link does not mean deleting the file from the disk! deleting the last link also deletes the file • to delete a file entry from a directory, use rm $ rm <file-name> • deletion requires two permissions: permission to write in the directory permission to search the directory (execute bit x set on the directory from which the file is deleted)"},{"t":"Symbolic links","x":"Symbolic links • limitations of hard links the link and the linked file must reside on the same file system (formatted disk) only root can create hard links to directories • symbolic link: a file containing the name of the referenced file (a string) • used to circumvent the limitations of hard links • created with the command ln and the flag –s $ ln –s /etc/profile ~/.system-wide-profile • in general, shell commands dereference the symbolic link exceptions: lstat, remove, rename, unlink, etc. e.g.: for the symbolic link created above $ rm ~/.system-wide-profile # deletes the symbolic link, not the source, i.e. /etc/profile $ ls –l ~/.system-wide-profile # displays the attributes of the symbolic link, not those of the source $ cat ~/.system-wide-profile # displays the content of /etc/profile"},{"t":"Working with directories","x":"Working with directories • created with mkdir, deleted with rmdir $ mkdir <directory> $ mkdir –p </directory>/<subdirectory> # also creates the missing # directories on the fly $ rmdir <directory> # Note: rmdir can delete EMPTY directories! • changing the current directory $ cd <directory> # changes the directory to <directory> $ cd #  cd $HOME $ cd - # changes the current directory to the previous directory • determining the current working directory (current working directory) $ pwd"}]},{"id":"c4","pdf":"course4.pdf","kind":"lecture","n":4,"title":"File tools, processes, IPC and signals","blurb":"grep and find, what a process is, shared memory vs message passing, pipes and FIFOs, signals.","topics":["file commands","wildcards","grep","find","processes","IPC","FIFOs","signals"],"ar":1.7778,"pages":[{"t":"Tools and Techniques in Computer Science","x":"Tools and Techniques in Computer Science Fall Semester 2026 Vlad Olaru"},{"t":"Lecture 4 - outline","x":"Lecture 4 - outline • files and directories (epilogue) • processes • inter-process communication • signals"},{"t":"Summary of useful commands for working with files","x":"Summary of useful commands for working with files • mkdir – creates directories • rmdir – deletes directories (provided they are empty) • touch – creates an empty file if it does not already exist • mv – moves directories or files renames; no physical copying takes place unless the data is moved from one disk to another • cp – copies (physically duplicates) directories (with –r) or files multiple directories/files can be copied into a destination directory $ cp <file-1> <file-2> …. <file-n> <directory> • rm – deletes directories (with –r) or files • ln – creates hard or symbolic links • mknod – creates special files of character, block or FIFO type $ mknod /dev/sda3 b 8 3 ‖ Summary of useful commands for working with files • mkdir – creates directories • rmdir – deletes directories (provided they are empty) • touch – creates an empty file if it does not already exist • mv – moves directories or files renames; no physical copying takes place unless the data is moved from one disk to another • cp – copies (physically duplicates) directories (with –r) or files multiple directories/files can be copied into a destination directory $ cp <file-1> <file-2> …. <file-n> <directory> • rm – deletes directories (with –r) or files • ln – creates hard or symbolic links • mknod – creates special files of character, block or FIFO type $ mknod /dev/sda3 b83"},{"t":"Wildcards","x":"Wildcards • special characters interpreted by the shell • ^ – denotes the beginning of the line • $ – denotes the end of the line • atom = a character or a set of characters grouped with [ ] or ( ) • * – 0 or more occurrences of the preceding atom • + – at least 1 occurrence of the preceding atom, possibly more • ? – at most one occurrence of the preceding atom (0 or 1 occurrences) ‖ Wildcards • special characters interpreted by the shell •^– denotes the beginning of the line •$– denotes the end of the line • atom = a character or a set of characters grouped with [ ] or ()•*–0 or more occurrences of the preceding atom •+– at least 1 occurrence of the preceding atom, possibly more •?– at most one occurrence of the preceding atom (0 or 1 occurrences)"},{"t":"Searching in files","x":"Searching in files • common command: grep • uses patterns and regular expressions • synopsis $ grep <expression> <files> • useful options -R / -r recursive search (with/without dereferencing symbolic links) -i case insensitive -n prints the number of the line on which the expression was found -w searches for an exact match of the given pattern (whole word) -v inverts the sense of matching (displays the non-matching lines) • e.g.: $ grep printf *.c $ grep –v –w printf *.[ch] $ ls –l | grep ^d"},{"t":"Searching in directories","x":"Searching in directories • common command: find • synopsis: $ find <pathname> -name <expression> • useful options -exec executes the specified command on the elements found -type restricts the displayed results to a given type (e.g., files) -iname similar to –name but case insensitive -maxdepth limits the recursion depth • e.g.: $ find / -name hello $ find . –iname \\*.o –exec rm {} \\;"},{"t":"Processes","x":"Processes • process = the abstraction of a program’s execution (instr. + data) • in fact, it is the image of the program in memory + its execution state at a given moment (reflected by the CPU registers and the values of the program’s variables) • identified by a Process ID (PID) • the ps command displays the PIDs of the currently running processes • several running instances of the same program are different processes, with different PIDs • process execution is sequential: there is no parallel execution of instructions within a single process • several parts the program code, also known as the text section the current state, reflected by the CPU registers the stack contains temporary data function parameters, return addresses, local variables the initialized/uninitialized data sections contain the global data the heap contains the memory dynamically allocated by the program at run time"},{"t":"Memory layout of a C program","x":"Memory layout of a C program program break -> environment variables -> 8 stack pointer ->"},{"t":"Inter-process communication (IPC)","x":"Inter-process communication (IPC) • processes can be independent or cooperating • cooperating processes can affect or be affected by other processes, including through data sharing • reasons for cooperation information sharing computation speedup modularity convenience • cooperating processes require inter-process communication (IPC) mechanisms • IPC models shared memory message passing"},{"t":"Communication models","x":"Communication models (a) Shared memory. (b) Message passing."},{"t":"IPC – Message Passing","x":"IPC – Message Passing • processes communicate with each other without shared variables • operations: send(message) receive(message) • the message size is fixed or variable"},{"t":"Types of communication","x":"Types of communication • direct: processes explicitly use each other’s identity send (P, message) – sends a message to process P receive(Q, message) – receives a message from process Q • indirect: messages are sent to and received from mailboxes (also called ports) each mailbox has a unique ID processes can communicate only if they share a mailbox operations create a mailbox (port) send and receive messages through the mailbox delete the mailbox communication primitives send(A, message) – sends a message to mailbox A receive(A, message) – receives a message from mailbox A"},{"t":"Synchronization","x":"Synchronization • message passing can be blocking or non-blocking • blocking mode is considered synchronous  blocking send – the sender is blocked until the message is received  blocking receive – the receiver is blocked until a message is available • non-blocking mode is considered asynchronous  non-blocking send – the sender sends the message and continues  non-blocking receive – the receiver gets:  a valid message, or  an empty message (null) • various combinations are possible  if send and receive are both blocking, we have a rendez-vous"},{"t":"FIFO communication in Unix","x":"FIFO communication in Unix • through anonymous or named pipes • anonymous pipes  a combination of direct/indirect communication  cooperating processes implicitly know each other’s identity  unidirectional communication $ cat hello.c| grep printf • named pipes (FIFOs)  named special files  indirect communication; cooperating processes do not know each other’s identity  created with the commands mknod/mkfifo  once created, used just like files, but with a FIFO access policy $ mknod myfifo p #  mkfifo myfifo $ cat < myfifo & # first there must be a # receiver waiting for data $ echo “write some message in myfifo” > myfifo ‖ FIFO communication in Unix • through anonymous or named pipes • anonymous pipes  a combination of direct/indirect communication  cooperating processes implicitly know each other’s identity  unidirectional communication $ cat hello.c| grep printf • named pipes (FIFOs)  named special files  indirect communication; cooperating processes do not know each other’s identity  created with the commands mknod/mkfifo  once created, used just like files, but with a FIFO access policy $ mknod myfifo p# mkfifo myfifo $ cat < myfifo & # first there must be a # receiver waiting for data $ echo “write some message in myfifo” > myfifo"},{"t":"Signals","x":"Signals • asynchronous notifications sent to a process about the occurrence of certain events • the software equivalent of exceptions (HW or SW) • sent either between processes or by the kernel to a process • generated from several sources: • system call kill(pid, signal) • the kill command: kill –TERM <pid> • keyboard: DEL, Ctrl-C, Ctrl-Z, etc. • certain software and hardware events generated by the kernel • the termination of a process is notified asynchronously to its parent with SIGCHLD • a process that performs a division by zero receives a SIGFPE signal • illegal memory access (e.g., unallocated memory) generates SIGSEGV"},{"t":"Signal handling","x":"Signal handling • the reaction of a process on receiving a signal depends on the type of the signal the program’s decision whether or not to handle the signaled event 1) the signal is ignored - typical for events that do not lead to errors/major consequences 2) program termination (the signal “kills” the process) - applies to the other events 3) the signal is handled according to the program’s instruction/disposition; the program is said to “catch” the signal - done by means of a signal handling routine (handler) Note: not every signal can be “caught”! E.g.: SIGKILL/SIGSTOP cannot be caught; KILL invariably terminates the program $ kill -KILL <pid> #  kill -9 <pid>"},{"t":"Examples of using signals","x":"Examples of using signals • the kill command uses a PID previously identified with the command ps $ kill –<signal-name/signal-no> <PID> $ kill –TERM 4899 #  kill 4899 or kill -15 4899 Note: the signal name can be given in full, e.g., SIGTERM, or abbreviated, e.g., TERM $ kill –l # displays all available signals $ kill –l 15 # maps a signal number to its name"},{"t":"Examples of using signals (cont’d)","x":"Examples of using signals (cont’d) • signals can be generated voluntarily by the user, e.g.: • Ctrl-c generates SIGINT usually terminates the process run by the shell if the process catches SIGINT, pressing Ctrl-c executes the signal handler associated by the process with SIGINT (i.e., the process does not die automatically) $ kill –SIGINT 4899 #  kill -2 4899 • Ctrl-z generates SIGTSTP suspends the execution of the current process (it can be resumed with job control commands, fg/bg) $ kill –SIGTSTP 4899 #  kill -20 4899"}]},{"id":"c5","pdf":"course5.pdf","kind":"lecture","n":5,"title":"Shell programming","blurb":"Scripts from the ground up: variables, expansion, quoting, tests, conditionals, loops, functions, read and debugging.","topics":["variables","expansion","quoting","scripts & #!","special variables","test / [ ]","if / case","loops","functions"],"ar":1.7778,"pages":[{"t":"Tools and Techniques in Computer Science","x":"Tools and Techniques in Computer Science Fall Semester 2026 Vlad Olaru"},{"t":"Lecture 5 - outline","x":"Lecture 5 - outline • shell programming • scripts • variables • tests • conditional statements • iterative and repetitive statements • functions"},{"t":"Motivation","x":"Motivation • we often want a single command for a sequence of commands • the commands are mostly shell or system commands, not logical or arithmetic operations • rapid development of short programs (in general) • in particular, used extensively by operating systems at startup/shutdown (e.g., for services) & for administration • portability: we want them to work on other systems as well, in order to automate configuration or administration tasks • sources: internet tutorials (e.g., CS2043 Unix and Scripting at Cornell University)"},{"t":"Shell variants","x":"Shell variants • sh(1) – Bourne shell • among the first shells • available everywhere at /bin/sh • reduced functionality • portability • csh(1) – C shell, with commands close to the C way of working • ksh(1) – Korn shell, based on sh(1), successor of csh(1) • bash(1) – Bourne Again shell • the most widespread • also available in Windows 10 • most modern scripts are written in bash(1) • this has led to a lack of portability • large project with many lines of code and security issues"},{"t":"Shell variables","x":"Shell variables • two types of variables: local and environment variables • local variables exist only in the current instance of the shell • convention: local variables are written in lowercase • assignment: x=1 (no spaces!) • x = 1: interpreted as the command x with the arguments = and 1 • the value of a variable is used by prefixing its name with $ • the content of any variable can be displayed with the command echo(1) $ x=1 $ echo $ x 1 ‖ Shell variables • two types of variables: local and environment variables • local variables exist only in the current instance of the shell • convention: local variables are written in lowercase • assignment: x=1 (no spaces!) •x= 1: interpreted as the command x with the arguments = and 1 • the value of a variable is used by prefixing its name with $ • the content of any variable can be displayed with the command echo(1) $ x=1 $ echo $x1"},{"t":"Environment variables","x":"Environment variables • used by the system to define how programs operate • the shell passes environment variables to its child processes • env(1) – displays all the environment variables that are set • important variables • $HOME – the directory where the current user’s data is kept • $PATH – list of directories in which executables are searched for • $SHELL – the default shell program • $EDITOR – the default file editor • $LANG – the default language (e.g. ro_RO.UTF-8) • export(1) – used to set an environment variable • printenv(1) – used to display an environment variable $ ex p o r t V=2 $ p r i n t e n v V 2 $ echo $ V 2 ‖ Environment variables • used by the system to define how programs operate • the shell passes environment variables to its child processes • env(1) – displays all the environment variables that are set • important variables • $HOME – the directory where the current user’s data is kept • $PATH – list of directories in which executables are searched for • $SHELL – the default shell program • $EDITOR – the default file editor • $LANG – the default language (e.g. ro_RO.UTF-8) • export(1) – used to set an environment variable • printenv(1) – used to display an environment variable $ ex port V=2 $printenvV2$ echo $V2"},{"t":"Local vs environment variables","x":"Local vs environment variables Local variable $ V = 2 $ echo $ V 2 $ bash bash$ echo $ V bash$ Environment variable $ V = 2 $ echo $ V 2 $ export V $ bash bash$ echo $ V 2 ‖ Local vs environment variables Local variable $V=2$ echo $V2$ bash bash$ echo $ V bash$ Environment variable $V=2$ echo $V2$ export V $ bash bash$ echo $V2"},{"t":"Variable expansion","x":"Variable expansion Variables can be more than simple scalars • $(cmd) – first evaluates the command cmd, and the result becomes the value of the variable $ echo $ ( pwd ) /home/ s t u d e n t $ x= $ ( f i n d . −name \\ ∗. c ) $ echo $ x . / b a t l e f t . c . / p c i e . c . / maxint . c . / e i s a i d . c • $((expr)) – first evaluates the arithmetic expression + side effects $ x=1 $ echo $ ( ( 1 + 1 )) 2 $ echo $ ( ( x +1) ) 2 $ echo $ ( ( x <1) ) 0 $ echo $ ( ( x + + ) ) 1 $ echo $ ( ( x + + ) ) 2 $ echo $( ( + + x )) 4 ‖ Variable expansion Variables can be more than simple scalars • $(cmd) – first evaluates the command cmd, and the result becomes the value of the variable $ echo $ ( pwd ) /home/ student$ x= $(find. −name \\ ∗. c)$ echo $x./batleft.c./pcie.c./ maxint .c./eisaid.c• $((expr)) – first evaluates the arithmetic expression + side effects $ x=1 $ echo $((1+1 )) 2 $ echo $((x +1) )2$ echo $((x <1) )0$ echo $((x++))1$ echo $((x++))2$ echo $( (++x )) 4"},{"t":"Quoting","x":"Quoting Character strings are interpreted differently depending on quoting: • Single quotes ‘’ • all characters keep their literal value • the character ’ cannot appear in the string, not even preceded by \"\\\" • example: • $ echo ’I have a variable $ x ’ I have a variable $ x • Double quotes \"\" • special characters $ ‘ \\ (optional !) • the other characters keep their literal value • example: • $ echo \" $ USER has home i n $ HOME\" student has home i n /home/ s t u d e n t ‖ Quoting Character strings are interpreted differently depending on quoting: • Single quotes ‘’ • all characters keep their literal value • the character ’ cannot appear in the string, not even preceded by \"\\\" • example: • $ echo ’I have a variable $x’I have a variable $x• Double quotes \"\" • special characters $‘\\ (optional !) • the other characters keep their literal value • example: • $ echo \" $ USER has home in$ HOME\" student has home i n /home/ student"},{"t":"Quoting (cont.)","x":"Quoting (cont.) Character strings are interpreted differently depending on quoting: • Back quotes ` work like $() $ echo \" Today i s `date ` \" Today i s Wed May 2 18: 00: 08 EEST 2018"},{"t":"Structure of bash commands","x":"Structure of bash commands • pipelines $ cmd1 | cmd2 | … |cmdn # parallel execution of commands $ cmd1 |& cmd2 |& … |& cmdn # “|&” is equivalent to “2>&1 |” • command lists $ cmd1; cmd2; …; cmdn # sequential execution of commands $ cmd1 && cmd2 && … && cmdn # conditional execution of commands $ cmd1 || cmd2 …. || cmdn • the bash variable “?” holds the termination code (exit status) of the last executed command (zero means success) $ echo $? Note: ? is not an environment variable!"},{"t":"Examples of pipelines and command lists","x":"Examples of pipelines and command lists • pipelines $ l s −lR | t ee f i l e s . l s t | wc −l • command lists $ l s −lR `pwd` > f i l e s . l s t ; wc −l f i l e s . l s t • conditional commands $ mkdir ac te && mv ∗. docx a c te / $ c h s h 2 > / d e v / n u l l && grep `whoami` /etc/passwd || echo \" chsh f a i l e d !\" ‖ Examples of pipelines and command lists • pipelines $ls −lR | t ee files.lst| wc −l • command lists $ls −lR `pwd` >files.lst; wc −l files.lst• conditional commands $ mkdir ac te && mv ∗. docx a c te /$chsh2>/dev/null && grep `whoami` /etc/passwd || echo \" chsh failed !\""},{"t":"Scripting","x":"Scripting Script = a program written for a specific run-time environment that automates the execution of commands which could alternatively be executed manually by a human operator. • does not require compilation • execution is performed directly from the source code • this is why the programs that execute scripts are called interpreters rather than compilers • examples: perl, ruby, python, sed, awk, ksh, csh, bash"},{"t":"Interpreter directives","x":"Interpreter directives • indicated by the string #! on the first line of the script • has the form #!/path/to/interpreter • examples: #!/bin/sh, #!/usr/bin/python • for portability, use env(1) to find where the interpreter is installed • examples: #!/usr/bin/env ruby #!/usr/bin/env perl • anywhere else in the script, # is interpreted as the start of a comment and the rest of the line is ignored (equivalent to // in C) • introduced by Dennis Ritchie about 1979"},{"t":"Example: hello.sh","x":"Example: hello.sh 1. write the file hello.sh with the desired commands # ! / bi n / sh # Greet the us er echo \" Hello, $ USER! \" 2. allow execution: chmod +x hello.sh 3. run: $ . / h el l o . sh Hello, s t u d e n t ! Alternatively: $ sh h e l l o . sh# does not require execute permission ‖ Example: hello.sh 1. write the file hello.sh with the desired commands #!/ bi n / sh # Greet the us er echo \" Hello, $ USER! \" 2. allow execution: chmod +x hello.sh 3. run: $./h el lo. sh Hello, student! Alternatively: $ sh hello. sh# does not require execute permission"},{"t":"Special variables in scripts","x":"Special variables in scripts • $1, $2, ..., ${10}, ${11}, ... – the arguments in the order received • if the argument number has more than two digits, it must be enclosed in braces • $0 – the name of the script (e.g. hello.sh) • $# – the number of arguments received • $* – all the arguments, written as \"$1 $2 . . . $n\" • $@ – all the arguments, written as \"$1\" \"$2\" . . . \"$n\" • $? – the exit value of the last executed command • $$ – the ID of the current process • $! – the ID of the last process suspended from execution • shifting the positional parameters to the left: the command shift ‖ Special variables in scripts • $1, $2, ..., ${10}, ${11}, ... – the arguments in the order received • if the argument number has more than two digits, it must be enclosed in braces • $0 – the name of the script (e.g. hello.sh) • $# – the number of arguments received • $* – all the arguments, written as \"$1 $2 ... $n\" • $@ – all the arguments, written as \"$1\" \"$2\" ... \"$n\" • $? – the exit value of the last executed command • $$ – the ID of the current process • $! – the ID of the last process suspended from execution • shifting the positional parameters to the left: the command shift"},{"t":"Examples: script arguments","x":"Examples: script arguments • add.sh – adds two numbers #!/ b i n / sh echo $(($ 1 + $ 2)) call: $ sh add . sh 1 2 3 Variant add2.sh: • # ! / bin / sh sum = $ 1 shift echo $(($ sum + $ 1)) ‖ Examples: script arguments • add.sh – adds two numbers #!/ bin/ sh echo $(($ 1+$ 2)) call: $ sh add . sh 123 Variant add2.sh: •#!/ bin / sh sum =$1 shift echo $(($ sum + $ 1))"},{"t":"Examples: script arguments","x":"Examples: script arguments • tolower.sh – mimics the C functiontolower(3) # ! / bi n / sh t r ’ [A−Z ] ’ ’ [ a−z ] ’ < $ 1 > $ 2 call: $ echo \"WHERE ARE YOU?\" > s cre a m i n g . t x t $ . / t ol ower . sh scr eami ng . t x t decent . t x t $ ca t decent . t x t where a r e you ? ‖ Examples: script arguments • tolower.sh – mimics the C functiontolower(3) #!/ bi n / sh tr’ [A−Z ]’’[ a−z ]’<$1>$2 call: $ echo \"WHERE ARE YOU?\" > s cre aming.txt$./t ol ower . sh scr eami ng .txt decent .txt$ ca t decent .txt where are you ?"},{"t":"Control blocks","x":"Control blocks For more complex scripts we need, as in any language, control blocks • conditional – i f , test [ ], case • iterative – for, while, until • comparison – -ne, - lt, -gt • functions – function • exits – break, continue, return, exit ‖ Control blocks For more complex scripts we need, as in any language, control blocks • conditional –if, test [ ], case • iterative – for, while, until • comparison – -ne, - lt, -gt • functions – function • exits – break, continue, return, exit"},{"t":"If","x":"If • keywords: if, then, elif, else, fi • e.g.: if test-cmd then cmds elif test-cmd then cmds else cmds fi • the result of test-cmd appears in $? (0 –> true, !=0 ->false)"},{"t":"Example: if","x":"Example: if Searches a file for data; appends it if not found # ! / b i n / sh i f grep \" $ 1 \" $ 2 > / dev / n u l l then echo \" $ 1 found i n f i l e $ 2 \" e l s e echo \" $ 1 not found i n f i l e $ 2 , appending \" echo \" $ 1 \" > > $ 2 fi Call: $ . / t e x t . sh who d ece n t . t x t who not found i n f i l e d ece n t . txt , appending $ ca t d ece n t . t x t where a r e you ? who ‖ Example: if Searches a file for data; appends it if not found #!/bin/ sh i f grep \"$1\"$2>/ dev /null then echo \"$1 found infile$2\"else echo \"$1 not found infile$2, appending \" echo \"$1\">>$2 fi Call: $./text. sh who d ece nt.txt who not found infiled ece nt. txt , appending $ ca t d ece nt.txt where are you ? who"},{"t":"or test [ ]","x":"testor [ ] • we do not always want to test the result of executing a command • there are expressions for comparing or checking variables • test expr – evaluates the truth value of expr • [ expr ] – performs the same operation (mind the spaces!) • [ is a built-in command $ type [ [ is a shell builtin • expressions can be combined logically using • [ expr1 -a expr2 ] – conjunction • [ expr1 -o expr2 ] – disjunction • [ ! expr ] – negation ‖ testor []• we do not always want to test the result of executing a command • there are expressions for comparing or checking variables • test expr – evaluates the truth value of expr • [ expr ] – performs the same operation (mind the spaces!) • [ is a built-in command $ type [ [ is a shell builtin • expressions can be combined logically using • [ expr1 -a expr2 ] – conjunction • [ expr1 -o expr2 ] – disjunction •[! expr ] – negation"},{"t":"Test expressions: numbers","x":"Test expressions: numbers • [ n1 -eq n2 ] – n1 = n2 • [ n1 -ne n2 ] – n1 /= n2 • [ n1 -ge n2 ] – n1 ≥n2 • [ n1 -gt n2 ] – n1 >n2 • [ n1 -l e n2 ] – n1 ≤n2 • [ n1 - l t n2 ] – n1 <n2 ‖ Test expressions: numbers • [ n1 -eq n2 ] – n1 = n2 • [ n1 -ne n2 ] – n1 /= n2 • [ n1 -ge n2 ] – n1 ≥n2 • [ n1 -gt n2 ] – n1 >n2 • [ n1 -l e n2 ] – n1 ≤n2 • [ n1 -lt n2 ] – n1 <n2"},{"t":"Test expressions: character strings","x":"Test expressions: character strings • [ str ] – str has non-zero length • [ -n str ] – str is not empty • [ -z str ] – str is empty (\"\") • [ str1 = str2 ] – identical strings • [ str1 == str2 ] – identical strings • [ str1 != str2 ] – different strings"},{"t":"Test expressions: files","x":"Test expressions: files • -e path – checks for the existence of the path path • - f path – checks whether path is a regular file • -d path – checks whether path is a directory • -r path – checks whether you have permission to read path • -w path – checks whether you have permission to write path • -x path – checks whether you have permission to execute path ‖ Test expressions: files • -e path – checks for the existence of the path path •-f path – checks whether path is a regular file • -d path – checks whether path is a directory • -r path – checks whether you have permission to read path • -w path – checks whether you have permission to write path • -x path – checks whether you have permission to execute path"},{"t":"while","x":"while • executes the block as long as the command cmd executes successfully wh i l e cmd do cmd1 cmd2 done • instead of cmd, we can use a test expression • on a single line: while cmd; do cmd1; cmd2; done ‖ while • executes the block as long as the command cmd executes successfully wh ile cmd do cmd1 cmd2 done • instead of cmd, we can use a test expression • on a single line: while cmd; do cmd1; cmd2; done"},{"t":"Example: while","x":"Example: while Displays all numbers from 1 to 10: i =1 wh i l e [ $ i −l e 10 ] do echo \" $i \" i= $( ( $i +1)) done Or on a single line: i=1; while [ $i -l e 10 ] ; do echo \" $ i \"; i=$(($i+1)); done ‖ Example: while Displays all numbers from 1 to 10: i =1 wh ile[$i −l e 10 ] do echo \" $i \" i= $( ( $i +1)) done Or on a single line: i=1; while [ $i -l e 10 ] ; do echo \"$i \"; i=$(($i+1)); done"},{"t":"until","x":"until • executes the block as long as the command cmd executes unsuccessfully until cmd do cmd1 cmd2 done • instead of a command, we can use a test expression • on a single line: until cmd; do cmd1 ; cmd2; done"},{"t":"for","x":"for • executes the block for each value in the list f o r v ar i n s t r 1 s t r 2 . . . st r N do cmd1 cmd2 … done • var takes the values str1, str2 … strN • usually the commands in the block (cmd1, cmd2) depend on var • the for command has several forms; this is the most common one • on a single line: for var in str1 str2 . . . strN; do cmd1 ; cmd2; done for ‖ for • executes the block for each value in the list forv ar instr1str2... st r N do cmd1 cmd2 … done • var takes the values str1, str2 … strN • usually the commands in the block (cmd1, cmd2) depend on var • the for command has several forms; this is the most common one • on a single line: for var in str1 str2 ... strN; do cmd1 ; cmd2; done for"},{"t":"Example: for","x":"Example: for Compiles all C files in the current directory f o r f i n ∗. c do echo \" $f \" cc $f −o \" $f . out\" done Or on a single line: for f in * . c ; do echo \"$f\"; cc $f -o $f.out; done ‖ Example: for Compiles all C files in the current directory forfin ∗. c do echo \" $f \" cc $f −o \" $f . out\" done Or on a single line: for f in *.c; do echo \"$f\"; cc $f -o $f.out; done"},{"t":"loop – traditional form for","x":"forloop – traditional form • a form found only in some shells, not portable • executes the block using a C-like syntax f o r (( i =1; i <=10; i + + )) do echo $ i done • i takes in turn all the values between 1 and 10 • in the example, the block displays $i, but any number of other commands may appear • on a single line: for (( i=1; i<=10; i++ ) ) ; do cmd1; cmd2; done ‖ forloop – traditional form • a form found only in some shells, not portable • executes the block using a C-like syntax for (( i =1; i <=10; i++ )) do echo $ i done • i takes in turn all the values between 1 and 10 • in the example, the block displays $i, but any number of other commands may appear • on a single line: for (( i=1; i<=10; i++ )); do cmd1; cmd2; done"},{"t":"Case","x":"Case • behaves similarly to switch in C ca se v ar i n p a t t er n 1 ) cmds ; ; p a t t er n 2 ) cmds ; ; ∗) def aul tcm d ; ; es a c • pattern – a string or any shell expression (similar to the wildcards used for grep(1)) ‖ Case • behaves similarly to switch in C ca se v ar inpatt er n1) cmds ;;patt er n2) cmds ; ; ∗) def aul tcm d;; es ac• pattern – a string or any shell expression (similar to the wildcards used for grep(1))"},{"t":"Example: case","x":"Example: case echo “Greetings !” read input_string case $input_string in hi) echo “A good day to you too !” ;; salut) echo “Buna ziua !” echo “Ou, peut-etre, bonjour a vous aussi ? “ ;; moin) echo “Guten morgen !” ;; *) echo “Sorry, I don’t understand !” ;; esac"},{"t":"Functions","x":"Functions • return values: • by changing the value of variables • using the exit command to terminate the script • using the return command to terminate the function • by printing results to stdout • retrieved by the caller in the manner typical of shells e.g., var=`expr …` • cannot modify the call parameters, but can modify global parameters"},{"t":"Function example","x":"Function example #!/bin/sh add_host() { IP_ADDR=$1 HOSTNAME=$2 shift; shift; ALIASES=$@ echo “$IP_ADDR \\t $HOSTNAME \\t $ALIASES” >> $hostfile } # the script starts here hostfile=$1 echo “Start script” add_host 80.96.21.88 fmi.unibuc.ro fmi add_host 80.96.21.209 www.unibuc.ro www Note: add_host is parsed by the shell and its syntax is checked, but it is executed only when called"},{"t":"Variable scope","x":"Variable scope • except for parameters, variables have no scope e.g. scope.sh #!/bin/sh somefn() { echo “Function call parameters are $@” a=10 } echo “script arguments are $@” a=11 echo “a is $a” somefn first string next echo “a is $a” Call: $ ./scope.sh 1 2 3 script arguments are 1 2 3 a is 11 Function call parameters are first string next a is 10 ‖ Variable scope • except for parameters, variables have no scope e.g. scope.sh #!/bin/sh somefn() { echo “Function call parameters are $@” a=10 } echo “script arguments are $@” a=11 echo “a is $a” somefn first string next echo “a is $a” Call: $ ./scope.sh 123 script arguments are 123a is 11 Function call parameters are first string next a is 10"},{"t":"Recursion","x":"Recursion • factorial.sh: #!/bin/sh factorial() { if [ “$1” –gt “1” ]; then prev=`expr $1 – 1` rec=`factorial $prev` val=`expr $1 \\* $rec` echo $val else echo 1 fi } echo –n “Input some number: ” read n factorial $n"},{"t":"Function libraries","x":"Function libraries • a collection of functions grouped in a file that DOES NOT BEGIN with the special line introduced by “#!” ! • global variables can be defined • the actual “call” is made using the command source e.g., renamelib.sh: MSG=“Renaming files …” rename() { mv $1 $2 } Calling the library function in the script libcall.sh: #!/bin/sh . ./renamelib.sh # equivalent to “source ./renamelib.sh” echo $MSG rename $1 $2"},{"t":"Return codes","x":"Return codes #!/bin/sh add_host() { if [ “$#” -eq “0” ]; then return 1 fi IP_ADDR=$1 HOSTNAME=$2 shift; shift; ALIASES=$@ echo “$IP_ADDR \\t $HOSTNAME \\t $ALIASES” >> $hostfile } hostfile=$1 echo “Start script” add_host RET_CODE=$? if [ “$RET_CODE” –eq “1” ]; then echo “No arguments to the add_host call !” fi"},{"t":"read","x":"read • reads one or more variables from stdin • syntax: read var1 var2 . . . varN $ read x y 1 2 $ echo $ x $ y 1 2 • with no variable, puts the entire result into $REPLY $ read h el l o $ echo $ REPLY h el l o • reading a file line by line: cat foo.txt | while read LINE; do echo $LINE; done ‖ read • reads one or more variables from stdin • syntax: read var1 var2 ... varN $ read xy12$ echo $x$y12• with no variable, puts the entire result into $REPLY $ read h el lo$ echo $ REPLY h el lo• reading a file line by line: cat foo.txt | while read LINE; do echo $LINE; done"},{"t":"Debugging","x":"Debugging To debug a script, invoke it with the shell in use and the option - x. The executed commands appear on screen prefixed with + . $ sh −x t ol ower . sh s cr ea mi ng . t x t decent . t x t + t r [A−Z ] [ a−z ] + < scr eamin g . t x t + > decent . t x t ‖ Debugging To debug a script, invoke it with the shell in use and the option - x. The executed commands appear on screen prefixed with +.$ sh −x t ol ower . sh s cr ea mi ng .txt decent .txt+tr [A−Z ] [ a−z ]+< scr eamin g.txt+> decent .txt"}]},{"id":"c6","pdf":"course6.pdf","kind":"lecture","n":6,"title":"Filters and text processing","blurb":"The classic text toolbox: cut, paste, split, join, bc, tr, sed and awk.","topics":["cut","paste","split","join","bc","tr","sed","awk"],"ar":1.7778,"pages":[{"t":"Tools and Techniques in Computer Science","x":"Tools and Techniques in Computer Science Fall Semester 2026 Vlad Olaru"},{"t":"Lecture 6 - outline","x":"Lecture 6 - outline • shell programming • filters • automatic text editing • automatic text processing"},{"t":"Text processing: cmd1 | filter | cmd2","x":"Text processing: cmd1 | filter | cmd2 Vocabulary • filter – a program, command or operation that processes the text output of a command so that the new form can be further processed by the user or by another program • line – a string of characters terminated by \\n (or \\r\\n in Windows) • separator (or delimiter) – a character or string used to delimit the data within a line • field – a substring of a line that represents a certain type of data • example: • line: surname firstname group series • separator: \" \" • fields: surname, first name, group and series ‖ Text processing: cmd1 | filter | cmd2 Vocabulary • filter – a program, command or operation that processes the text output of a command so that the new form can be further processed by the user or by another program • line – a string of characters terminated by \\n (or \\r\\n in Windows) • separator (or delimiter) – a character or string used to delimit the data within a line • field – a substring of a line that represents a certain type of data • example: • line: surname firstname group series • separator: \"\"• fields: surname, first name, group and series"},{"t":"cut(1)","x":"cut(1) cut(1) extracts fields from each input line • l i s t – numbers or ranges separated by commas or spaces • -b l i s t – the list contains byte positions • -c l i s t – the list contains character positions • - f l i s t – the list specifies fields • -d delim – specifies the field delimiter (the default is \\t) • -n – do not split multi-byte characters into bytes • -s – skips lines that do not contain delimiters Invocation forms • cut -b [-n] l i s t [ f i l e . . . ] • cut -c l i s t [ f i l e . . . ] • cut - f l i s t [-s ] [-d delim] [ f i l e . . . ] ‖ cut(1) cut(1) extracts fields from each input line •list– numbers or ranges separated by commas or spaces • -b list– the list contains byte positions • -c list– the list contains character positions •-flist– the list specifies fields • -d delim – specifies the field delimiter (the default is \\t) • -n – do not split multi-byte characters into bytes • -s – skips lines that do not contain delimiters Invocation forms • cut -b [-n] list[file...]• cut -c list[file...]• cut -flist [-s ] [-d delim] [file...]"},{"t":"Examples: cut(1)","x":"Examples: cut(1) Displays the names and shells of the users of the system: $ cut - d : - f 1, 7 /etc/ passwd nobody : / sbin / nologin paul : / bin /ksh build : / bin /ksh joe : / bin /ksh _mysql : / sbin / nologin _ p o s t g re s q l : / bin /sh souser : / bin /ksh alex : / bin /ksh ‖ Examples: cut(1) Displays the names and shells of the users of the system: $ cut -d:-f 1, 7 /etc/ passwd nobody : / sbin / nologin paul : / bin /ksh build : / bin /ksh joe : / bin /ksh _mysql : / sbin / nologin _postg re sql:/ bin /sh souser : / bin /ksh alex : / bin /ksh"},{"t":"Examples: cut(1)","x":"Examples: cut(1) Shows the names and login dates of the active users: $ who | cut - c 1 - 8 ,18 - 30 May 8 18:32 May 3 20:39 Apr 28 Apr 19 Apr 19 06:47 14:22 08:24 deraadt dlg j s i n g landry deraadt ke t te n i s deraadt p i r o f t i May 9 02:47 May 3 22:18 May 9 04:36 ‖ Examples: cut(1) Shows the names and login dates of the active users: $ who | cut -c1-8 ,18 - 30 May 8 18:32 May 3 20:39 Apr 28 Apr 19 Apr 19 06:47 14:22 08:24 deraadt dlg jsing landry deraadt ke t te nis deraadt pirofti May 9 02:47 May 3 22:18 May 9 04:36"},{"t":"paste(1)","x":"paste(1) Pastes the input files together as columns (vertically) • -d l i s t – uses the delimiter to replace the newline character \\n of each file • -s – serialization • ' - ' – stands for standard input (stdin) Invocation • paste [-s ] [-d l i s t ] f i l e . . . ‖ paste(1) Pastes the input files together as columns (vertically) • -d list– uses the delimiter to replace the newline character \\n of each file • -s – serialization •'-'– stands for standard input (stdin) Invocation • paste [-s ] [-d list]file..."},{"t":"Example: paste(1)","x":"Example: paste(1) Consider the files with first names and last names: $ echo \" Paul\\ nAlex \\ nAna \" > f i r s t n a m e s . t x t $ echo \" I r o f t i \\ nAlexandrescu \\ nPopescu \" \\ > l a s t n a m e s . t x t By default, paste(1) joins the first and last names $ paste Paul Alex Ana f i r s t n a m e s . t x t lastnames. t x t I r o f t i Alexandrescu Popescu The same, but with serialization $ paste - s f i r s t n a m e s . t x t l a s t n a m e s . t x t Paul Alex Ana I r o f t i Alexandrescu Popescu ‖ Example: paste(1) Consider the files with first names and last names: $ echo \" Paul\\ nAlex \\ nAna \">firstnames.txt$ echo \"Irofti\\ nAlexandrescu \\ nPopescu \"\\>lastnames.txt By default, paste(1) joins the first and last names $ paste Paul Alex Ana firstnames.txt lastnames. txtIrofti Alexandrescu Popescu The same, but with serialization $ paste -sfirstnames.txtlastnames.txt Paul Alex Ana Irofti Alexandrescu Popescu"},{"t":"Example: paste(1)","x":"Example: paste(1) Lists current-directory files in three columns: $ l s | paste - - - 1 10 11 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9 intro Identical to the call l s | paste -s -d ’\\t\\t\\n’ - Creates a list of the system’s bin directories, separated by : $ f i n d / - name bin - type d | paste - s - d : - /usr/ X11R6/bin : / usr/ l o c a l / bin : /usr/ l o c a l / l i b /qt4 /bin : . . . ‖ Example: paste(1) Lists current-directory files in three columns: $ls| paste ---1 10 11 23456789 intro Identical to the call ls| paste -s -d ’\\t\\t\\n’ - Creates a list of the system’s bin directories, separated by :$find/- name bin - type d | paste -s-d:- /usr/ X11R6/bin : / usr/ local/ bin : /usr/ local/lib /qt4 /bin :..."},{"t":"split(1)","x":"split(1) Splits the given file into several files of 1000 lines each. • -a suffix_length – how many letters the suffix of the new files contains • -b byte_count – creates files of the given length in bytes • - l line_count – creates files with the given number of lines • f i l e – the file to split; the default is stdin • name – the prefix for the new files Invocation • split [options] [ f i l e [name]] By default it creates the files xaa, xab, xac, ... ‖ split(1) Splits the given file into several files of 1000 lines each. • -a suffix_length – how many letters the suffix of the new files contains • -b byte_count – creates files of the given length in bytes •-l line_count – creates files with the given number of lines •file– the file to split; the default is stdin • name – the prefix for the new files Invocation • split [options] [file [name]] By default it creates the files xaa, xab, xac, ..."},{"t":"Example: split(1)","x":"Example: split(1) • Splits the LaTeX file into several files of 100 lines each: $ wc - l fisier. tex 362 fisier. tex $ s p l i t - l 100 fisier. tex $ l s x* xaa xab xac xad $ wc - l x* 100 xaa 100 xab 100 xac 62 xad 362 t o t a l The same, but with the prefix fisier anda single-letter suffix: $ s p l i t - a 1 - l 100 fisier. tex fisier ‖ Example: split(1) • Splits the LaTeX file into several files of 100 lines each: $ wc - l fisier. tex 362 fisier. tex $split-l 100 fisier. tex $ls x* xaa xab xac xad $ wc - l x* 100 xaa 100 xab 100 xac 62 xad 362 total The same, but with the prefix fisier anda single-letter suffix: $split-a1-l 100 fisier. tex fisier"},{"t":"join(1)","x":"join(1) Joins lines with identical keys from two different files • file1, file2 – the input files • -1 field – the key field in file 1 • -2 field – the key field in file 2 • -a file_number – produces a line for each unpairable line in the given file (1 or 2) Invocation • join [-1 field] [-2 field] [options] file1 file2"},{"t":"Example: join(1)","x":"Example: join(1) Joins the people for whom both age and income data exist: $ cat ag e . txt Paul 33 Alex 40 Ann 25 $ cat income . txt Paul 3000 Ann 4500 $ j o i n ag e . t x t income . txt Paul 33 3000 Ann 25 4500 The same, but also includes people (e.g. Alex) without income: $ j o i n - a1 a g e . t x t income . txt ‖ Example: join(1) Joins the people for whom both age and income data exist: $ cat ag e . txt Paul 33 Alex 40 Ann 25 $ cat income . txt Paul 3000 Ann 4500 $join ag e.txt income . txt Paul 33 3000 Ann 25 4500 The same, but also includes people (e.g. Alex) without income: $join- a1 age.txt income . txt"},{"t":"Basic Calculator – bc(1)","x":"Basic Calculator – bc(1) Calculator for arithmetic and logical operations • - l – enables floating-point operations • -e expr – evaluates the expression; there can be several • f i l e – reads expressions from a file • binary operators are the same as in C • the logical operators && , | | and! are available in some implementations but are not specified by the POSIX standard • a stand-alone language with control blocks (e.g. while) • by default it starts a specialized shell and waits for commands Invocation • bc [ - l ] [-e expr] [ f i l e ] ‖ Basic Calculator – bc(1) Calculator for arithmetic and logical operations •-l– enables floating-point operations • -e expr – evaluates the expression; there can be several •file– reads expressions from a file • binary operators are the same as in C • the logical operators && ,|| and! are available in some implementations but are not specified by the POSIX standard • a stand-alone language with control blocks (e.g. while) • by default it starts a specialized shell and waits for commands Invocation • bc [-l] [-e expr] [file]"},{"t":"bc(1) – functions","x":"bc(1) – functions • s(x) – sine • c(x) – cosine • e(x) – exponential • l(x) – logarithm • a(x) – arctangent • sqrt(x) – square root • scale=n – precision, n decimal digits • quit – exits the program"},{"t":"Example: bc(1)","x":"Example: bc(1) Basic operations: $ echo \"1/3\" | bc 0 $ echo \"1/3\" | bc - l .33333333333333333333 $ echo \"1 >3\" | bc - l 0 $ echo \"1 <3\" | bc - l 1 $ bc - l - e \" sqrt ( 2 ) \" - e quit 1.41421356237309504880 ‖ Example: bc(1) Basic operations: $ echo \"1/3\" | bc 0 $ echo \"1/3\" | bc - l .33333333333333333333 $ echo \"1 >3\" | bc -l0$ echo \"1 <3\" | bc -l1$ bc -l-e\" sqrt (2)\"-e quit 1.41421356237309504880"},{"t":"Example: bc(1)","x":"Example: bc(1) $ bc - l scale =4 sqrt (3) 1.7320 4* a(1) 3.1412 c(4* a ( 1 ) ) - 1.0000 c(4* s ( 1 ) ) - .9750 quit ‖ Example: bc(1) $ bc - l scale =4 sqrt (3) 1.7320 4* a(1) 3.1412 c(4* a(1))- 1.0000 c(4* s(1))- .9750 quit"},{"t":"Translate – tr(1)","x":"Translate – tr(1) tr(1) translates characters from stdin andprints the result to stdout string1, string2 – the characters of the first string are translated into those of the second string -C,-c – complements the set of characters in string1 -d – deletes the characters that appear in string1 -s – squeezes repeated translated characters according to the last operand (either string1 or string2) Invocation forms: • tr [-Ccs] string1 string2 • tr [-Cc] -d string1 • tr [-Cc] -s string1"},{"t":"Example: tr(1)","x":"Example: tr(1) Creates a list of the input words: $ echo \" Billy goes h o m e \" | t r - cs \" [ A- Za - z ] \" \"\\ n\" Billy goes home Puts the input in caps $ echo \" Billy g o e s h o m e \"| t r \" [ a - z ] \" \" [ A- Z]\" BILLY GOES HOME Extracts the character strings from a binary file: $ echo \" i n t main ( ) { return 0 ; } \" | cc - xc - o t e s t - $ cat t e s t | t r - cd \" [ : print : ] \" ELF > @@8@@@@ >>>>>>>>HH H XX X @@TTTPtd44eHH H . . . ‖ Example: tr(1) Creates a list of the input words: $ echo \" Billy goes home\"|tr- cs \" [ A- Za -z]\" \"\\ n\" Billy goes home Puts the input in caps $ echo \" Billy goeshome \"| tr\"[a-z]\"\"[ A- Z]\" BILLY GOES HOME Extracts the character strings from a binary file: $ echo \"int main (){ return 0;}\"| cc - xc -otest-$ cat test|tr- cd \"[: print :]\" ELF > @@8@@@@ >>>>>>>>HH H XX X @@TTTPtd44eHH H..."},{"t":"Stream EDitor – sed(1)","x":"Stream EDitor – sed(1) sed(1) is a full-fledged text editor; moreover, it is actually a programming language! • generally used for text substitutions and deletions • unlike tr(1), it uses regular expressions • mode of operation • scans the input line by line looking for a pattern • if it finds a character string matching the pattern, it applies the user-given function to it • the functions can be predefined (substitution, deletion) or written by the user Invocation: • sed [options] command [ f i l e . . . ] ‖ Stream EDitor – sed(1) sed(1) is a full-fledged text editor; moreover, it is actually a programming language! • generally used for text substitutions and deletions • unlike tr(1), it uses regular expressions • mode of operation • scans the input line by line looking for a pattern • if it finds a character string matching the pattern, it applies the user-given function to it • the functions can be predefined (substitution, deletion) or written by the user Invocation: • sed [options] command [file...]"},{"t":"Examples: sed(1)","x":"Examples: sed(1) Substitution s,pattern,text, $ echo \" Paul Alex Ann\" | sed s , Alex , Paul , Paul Paul Ann $ echo \" Paul Alex Alex Ann\" | sed s , Alex , Paul , Paul Paul Alex Ann Substitution s,pattern,text,flag $ echo \" Paul Alex Alex Ann\" | sed s , Alex , Paul , g Paul Paul Paul Ann $ echo \" Paul Alex Alex Ann\" | sed s , Alex , Paul ,2 Paul Alex Paul Ann $ echo \" Paul Alex Ann\" | sed s , Alex , Paul , wfile Paul Paul Ann $ cat f i l e Paul Paul Ann ‖ Examples: sed(1) Substitution s,pattern,text, $ echo \" Paul Alex Ann\" | sed s , Alex , Paul , Paul Paul Ann $ echo \" Paul Alex Alex Ann\" | sed s , Alex , Paul , Paul Paul Alex Ann Substitution s,pattern,text,flag $ echo \" Paul Alex Alex Ann\" | sed s , Alex , Paul , g Paul Paul Paul Ann $ echo \" Paul Alex Alex Ann\" | sed s , Alex , Paul ,2 Paul Alex Paul Ann $ echo \" Paul Alex Ann\" | sed s , Alex , Paul , wfile Paul Paul Ann $ cat file Paul Paul Ann"},{"t":"Example: sed(1)","x":"Example: sed(1) Deleting the lines that contain the pattern: $ echo \" Paul Alex \\ nAlex Ann\" | sed / Paul/ d Alex Ann If we only want to delete the character string: $ echo \" Paul Alex \\ nAlex Ann\" | sed s/ Paul // g Alex Alex Ann Applying it to certain lines only: $ echo \" Paul\\ nAlex \\ nAlex \\ nAnn \\ nPaul\\ nGeorge \" | \\ sed 1 ,3 s/ Paul // g Alex Alex Ann Paul George ‖ Example: sed(1) Deleting the lines that contain the pattern: $ echo \" Paul Alex \\ nAlex Ann\" | sed / Paul/ d Alex Ann If we only want to delete the character string: $ echo \" Paul Alex \\ nAlex Ann\" | sed s/ Paul // g Alex Alex Ann Applying it to certain lines only: $ echo \" Paul\\ nAlex \\ nAlex \\ nAnn \\ nPaul\\ nGeorge \"|\\ sed 1 ,3 s/ Paul // g Alex Alex Ann Paul George"},{"t":"Example: sed(1)","x":"Example: sed(1) Swapping two words using regular expressions $ echo \" Paul M c C a r t n e y \\ nRingo Starr \\ nGeorge Harrison \" | sed ’ s , ^ \\ ( [ A- Z] [ A- Za - z ] * \\ ) \\( [ A- Z] [ A- Za - z ] * \\ ) ,\\2 \\1 , ’ M c C a r t n e y Paul Starr R i n g o Harrison George • ˆ – matches a pattern at the beginning of the line • [A-Z] – must start with an uppercase letter • [A-Za-z]* – may continue with any number of lowercase or uppercase letters • () – delimit two expressions in the pattern; we use \\ so that they are not interpreted by the shell (escaping) • \\1 \\2 – the strings matched by the two expressions ‖ Example: sed(1) Swapping two words using regular expressions $ echo \" Paul McCartney\\ nRingo Starr \\ nGeorge Harrison \" | sed ’s,^\\([ A- Z] [ A- Za -z]*\\) \\( [ A- Z] [ A- Za -z]*\\) ,\\2 \\1 ,’McCartney Paul Starr Ringo Harrison George •ˆ– matches a pattern at the beginning of the line • [A-Z] – must start with an uppercase letter • [A-Za-z]* – may continue with any number of lowercase or uppercase letters • () – delimit two expressions in the pattern; we use \\ so that they are not interpreted by the shell (escaping) • \\1 \\2 – the strings matched by the two expressions"},{"t":"Examples: sed(1)","x":"Examples: sed(1) There are even games written in sed(1) http://aurelio.net/projects/sedarkanoid/"},{"t":"awk(1)","x":"awk(1) A programming language specialized in processing text data • Alfred Aho, Peter Weinberger, and Brian Kernighan (1970) • works on the pattern matching – function application principle, like sed(1) • checks more than one pattern pattern1 { commands } pattern2 { commands } • checks the current line against each given pattern, then moves on to the next line • Turing Complete Invocation: • awk [options] [program] f i l e . . . ‖ awk(1) A programming language specialized in processing text data • Alfred Aho, Peter Weinberger, and Brian Kernighan (1970) • works on the pattern matching – function application principle, like sed(1) • checks more than one pattern pattern1 { commands } pattern2 { commands } • checks the current line against each given pattern, then moves on to the next line • Turing Complete Invocation: • awk [options] [program] file..."},{"t":"Examples: awk(1)","x":"Examples: awk(1) Displays the lines that contain the pattern • $ echo \" Paul M c C a r t n e y \\ n Ringo Starr \\ n George Harrison \" | awk ’ / Ringo / { p r i n t } ’ Ringo Starr Counts how many times a string appears in a file: $ echo \" Ann and Dan \\ n D a n a t t h e s c h o o l \\ nAnn got lost\" | \\ > awk ’ BEGIN { p r i n t \" Finding A n n a n d D a n \"} > /[Dd]an/ { dan ++} > /[Aa]nn/ { ann++} > END { p r i n t \" Found D a n \" d a n \" times and A n n \" ann \" times ! \" } ’ Finding A n n a n d D a n Found D a n 2 times and Ann 2 times! The commands marked with BEGIN andEND are executed only once. ‖ Examples: awk(1) Displays the lines that contain the pattern • $ echo \" Paul McCartney\\n Ringo Starr \\ n George Harrison \" | awk ’ / Ringo /{print}’ Ringo Starr Counts how many times a string appears in a file: $ echo \" Ann and Dan \\nDanattheschool\\ nAnn got lost\" |\\> awk ’ BEGIN {print\" Finding AnnandDan \"} > /[Dd]an/ { dan ++} > /[Aa]nn/ { ann++} > END {print\" Found Dan\"dan\" times and Ann\" ann \" times !\"}’ Finding AnnandDan Found Dan2 times and Ann 2 times! The commands marked with BEGIN andEND are executed only once."},{"t":"Examples: awk(1)","x":"Examples: awk(1) Processing the fields of a file • $0 – refers to the whole line • $ 1 , $ 2 , . . . , $ ( 1 0 ) – refer to the individual fields • FS(field separator) – the separator; set to a space by default $ cat account.txt OP SUM IN 10 OUT 5 IN 20 IN 3 OUT 25 $ awk ’ b a l a n c e \" ; amount = 0} > BEGIN { p r in t \" Account > /IN/ { amount += $2 } > /OUT/ { amount - = $2 } > END { p r i n t amount \" USD\" } ’ a c c o u n t . t x t Account balance 3 USD ‖ Examples: awk(1) Processing the fields of a file • $0 – refers to the whole line •$1,$2,...,$(10)– refer to the individual fields • FS(field separator) – the separator; set to a space by default $ cat account.txt OP SUM IN 10 OUT 5 IN 20 IN 3 OUT 25 $ awk ’balance\"; amount = 0} > BEGIN {pr in t \" Account > /IN/ { amount += $2 } > /OUT/ { amount - = $2 } > END {print amount \" USD\" }’account.txt Account balance 3 USD"},{"t":"awk(1): variables and functions","x":"awk(1): variables and functions Useful variables • NF – the number of fields in the current line • NR – the number of lines read so far • FILENAME – the name of the input file Useful functions • toupper(),tolower() – uppercase, lowercase • exp(),log(),sin() – mathematical functions • length() – the length of the string • int() – the integer part"},{"t":"Examples: awk(1)","x":"Examples: awk(1) Displays all the words: $ echo \" Billy goes home\" | \\ awk ’ { for ( i = 1 ; i<= NF; i++) print $i}’ Billy goes home Users and their shells: $ awk ’ BEGIN { FS = \" : \" } > { p r i n t \" User \" $1 \" uses \" $7 \" s h e l l . \" } ’ > / e tc/ passwd User User User User User nobody uses / sbin / nologin s h e l l . paul uses / bin / ksh s h e l l . souser uses / bin / ksh s h e l l . _rsync uses / sbin / nologin s h e l l . alex uses / usr/ l o c a l / bin / bash s h e l l . ‖ Examples: awk(1) Displays all the words: $ echo \" Billy goes home\" | \\ awk ’ { for (i=1; i<= NF; i++) print $i}’ Billy goes home Users and their shells: $ awk ’ BEGIN { FS =\":\"}>{print\" User \" $1 \" uses \" $7 \"shell.\"}’>/e tc/ passwd User User User User User nobody uses / sbin / nologin shell. paul uses / bin / ksh shell. souser uses / bin / ksh shell. _rsync uses / sbin / nologin shell. alex uses / usr/ local/ bin / bash shell."},{"t":"Examples: awk(1)","x":"Examples: awk(1) Displays only the shell name for a given user: $ awk ’ BEGIN { FS = \" : \" } > / paul/ { cmd = \" basename\" $7 ; > cmd | g e t l i n e s h e l l ; > print \" User \"$1\" uses \"s h e l l \" s h e l l . \" > close ( cmd ) ; > } ’ / etc/ passwd User paul uses ksh s h e l l . Runs an external command and gets its result with getline. ‖ Examples: awk(1) Displays only the shell name for a given user: $ awk ’ BEGIN { FS =\":\"}>/ paul/ { cmd = \" basename\" $7 ; > cmd |getlineshell;> print \" User \"$1\" uses \"s hell\"shell.\"> close ( cmd );>}’/ etc/ passwd User paul uses ksh shell. Runs an external command and gets its result with getline."}]},{"id":"c7","pdf":"course7.pdf","kind":"lecture","n":7,"title":"System services","blurb":"How services are started and managed: System V init scripts, Upstart jobs and systemd units.","topics":["daemons","System V","rc directories","init.d scripts","Upstart","systemd","targets","unit files"],"ar":1.7778,"pages":[{"t":"Tools and Techniques in Computer Science","x":"Tools and Techniques in Computer Science Fall Semester 2026 Vlad Olaru"},{"t":"Lecture 7 - outline","x":"Lecture 7 - outline • the user perspective (recap) • the system perspective • services"},{"t":"The user perspective","x":"The user perspective - user access to the system - under the control of init - getty allocates a terminal to a user - login authenticates the user and launches a command interpreter connected to the previously allocated terminal - the command interpreter - executes commands or scripts - command: an executable program; when running, it is a child process of the interpreter - processes are the in-memory images of the programs on disk (executable files) - commands call the operating system services for: - file and directory management - process control - system administration - etc."},{"t":"The system perspective","x":"The system perspective - operating system services are accessible in two ways - directly, through the kernel (the system call interface) - indirectly, through specialized programs implemented in userspace - which ultimately also call the kernel - shell commands - call the kernel services directly, or - contact userspace servers to perform the service - servers - specialized programs that provide operating system services in user space - also called daemons (Unix terminology) - accessible in various ways through one form or another of IPC (Inter-Process Communication) - shared memory - message passing - e.g.: network services, firewall, printing, time management, security, etc."},{"t":"Services","x":"Services - system programs started when the operating system boots - either servers/daemons or programs that contribute to the proper functioning of the system and to providing the execution environment for user programs - technically, processes started by init - provide various system services to the user - directly, through servers/daemons - indirectly, by providing a suitable execution environment for user programs - in general, they run as long as the system is up (possibly restarted automatically if errors occur) - configured separately for each runlevel - standard operations: start, stop, reload configuration, show status - multiple access and management interfaces - System V (Unix) - Upstart (Linux implementation of init) - Systemd (recent Linux variant)"},{"t":"The System V interface","x":"The System V interface - configured in the file /etc/inittab in Unix systems (and older versions of Linux) - init reads the default runlevel from here (initdefault) - services grouped in rc (run commands) directories, one for each runlevel + one for the initial stage, right after boot /etc/rc0.d, /etc/rc1.d, /etc/rc2.d, …, /etc/rc6.d, /etc/rcS.d (in Unix, grouped in /etc/rc.d/) - each rc directory contains symbolic links to scripts in /etc/init.d that contain the services executed when the system enters that runlevel - the execution order is given by the lexicographic order of the service names - disabling a service in a given runlevel - rename the link to the script so that the new name starts with K + 2 digits $ ln –s ../init.d/ssh K01ssh - enabling a service in a given runlevel - rename the link to the script so that the new name starts with S + 2 digits $ ln –s ../init.d/rc.local S05rc.local ‖ The System V interface - configured in the file /etc/inittab in Unix systems (and older versions of Linux) - init reads the default runlevel from here (initdefault) - services grouped in rc (run commands) directories, one for each runlevel + one for the initial stage, right after boot /etc/rc0.d, /etc/rc1.d, /etc/rc2.d, …, /etc/rc6.d, /etc/rcS.d (in Unix, grouped in /etc/rc.d/) - each rc directory contains symbolic links to scripts in /etc/init.d that contain the services executed when the system enters that runlevel - the execution order is given by the lexicographic order of the service names - disabling a service in a given runlevel - rename the link to the script so that the new name starts with K+2 digits $ ln –s ../init.d/ssh K01ssh - enabling a service in a given runlevel - rename the link to the script so that the new name starts with S+2 digits $ ln –s ../init.d/rc.local S05rc.local"},{"t":"Example: rc3.d","x":"Example: rc3.d"},{"t":"Linux init.d scripts","x":"Linux init.d scripts - include at the beginning, after the interpreter specification, a header with configuration information (in Linux, defined according to the LSB, Linux Standard Base) - technically these are comments, but they are interpreted by init - enclosed between the following lines ### BEGIN INIT INFO ### END INIT INFO each line of the form # {keyword}: arg1 [arg2 …] service start/stop keywords define the runlevels in which the services must be started/stopped by default # Default-Start 2 3 4 5 # Default-Stop 0 1 6 ‖ Linux init.d scripts - include at the beginning, after the interpreter specification, a header with configuration information (in Linux, defined according to the LSB, Linux Standard Base) - technically these are comments, but they are interpreted by init - enclosed between the following lines ### BEGIN INIT INFO ### END INIT INFO each line of the form # {keyword}: arg1 [arg2 …] service start/stop keywords define the runlevels in which the services must be started/stopped by default # Default-Start 2345# Default-Stop 016"},{"t":"Commands for handling the SysV interface","x":"Commands for handling the SysV interface - automating the handling of the directories rc - various commands depending on the OS: chkconfig, update-rc.d/bum, etc. E.g.: Ubuntu, dry run $ update-rc.d –n ssh enable 2 3 4 5 - executing service operations from /etc/init.d $ service –status-all $ service ssh start # equivalent to /etc/init.d/ssh start $ service ssh stop # equivalent to /etc/init.d/ssh stop $ service ssh reload # equivalent to /etc/init.d/ssh reload $ service ssh restart # equivalent to /etc/init.d/ssh restart ‖ Commands for handling the SysV interface - automating the handling of the directories rc - various commands depending on the OS: chkconfig, update-rc.d/bum, etc. E.g.: Ubuntu, dry run $ update-rc.d –n ssh enable 2345- executing service operations from /etc/init.d $ service –status-all $ service ssh start # equivalent to /etc/init.d/ssh start $ service ssh stop # equivalent to /etc/init.d/ssh stop $ service ssh reload # equivalent to /etc/init.d/ssh reload $ service ssh restart # equivalent to /etc/init.d/ssh restart"},{"t":"Upstart","x":"Upstart - Linux implementation of init - event-driven - events are messages sent to services - service processes are called jobs and are configured in /etc/init - jobs are actions executed upon receiving certain messages (events) - the configured services take precedence over the services in /etc/init.d - changes to configuration files are monitored with inotify - the configuration files include information about starting/stopping jobs E.g.: start on runlevel [2345] stop on runlevel [!2345] other configuration information: respawn (job restarted automatically in case of abnormal termination), exec E.g., tty1.conf: … respawn exec /sbin/getty -8 38400 tty1"},{"t":"Upstart (cont.)","x":"Upstart (cont.) - standard events: - startup (System V compatibility, starts jobs with runlevel-type events) emitted at the startup of init - when jobs start/stop, init emits starting, started, stopping, stopped - operation (1) loads the job configuration from /etc/init (2) for a start event, runs the corresponding job (3) the created jobs generate new events which in turn start other jobs (4) this cycle continues until all the necessary jobs have finished - unlike System V, it also allows managing user sessions - user session mode - PID > 1 - service configuration files: $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/upstart, $HOME/.init/ $XDG_CONFIG_DIRS/upstart, /usr/share/upstart/sessions Ex: $ upstart -user ‖ Upstart (cont.) - standard events: - startup (System V compatibility, starts jobs with runlevel-type events) emitted at the startup of init - when jobs start/stop, init emits starting, started, stopping, stopped - operation (1) loads the job configuration from /etc/init (2) for a start event, runs the corresponding job (3) the created jobs generate new events which in turn start other jobs (4) this cycle continues until all the necessary jobs have finished - unlike System V, it also allows managing user sessions - user session mode - PID >1- service configuration files: $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/upstart, $HOME/.init/ $XDG_CONFIG_DIRS/upstart, /usr/share/upstart/sessions Ex: $ upstart -user"},{"t":"Initctl","x":"Initctl - command for working with upstart - viewing jobs $ initctl list # list of jobs and their associated state $ initctl status networking # viewing a specific job • job state: a pair goal/current state the goal defines the desired operation (start, stop, etc.) the pair is updated as the jobs evolve - starting/stopping jobs $ initctl start networking $ initctl stop networking - manually emitting an event (message) $ initctl emit some_event"},{"t":"Systemd","x":"Systemd - the recent version of init for Linux - uses targets instead of runlevels to start the system services - targets have dependencies that must be satisfied - the dependency system manages 12 types of units - unit - encapsulates various objects relevant for system booting and maintenance - described in configuration files - can be active/inactive, similar to processes (they also have intermediate states) - e.g. unit types - service units – start and control the daemons - target units – group units or provide synchronization points during boot - device units – expose devices in systemd and enable device-based activation - mount units – control the mount points of the file system - timer units – useful for activating other units based on timers - etc."},{"t":"Systemd (cont.)","x":"Systemd (cont.) - services are not started in a fixed order as in System V, but based on unit dependencies - just like upstart, it can be started in user mode (PID > 1) E.g.: $ systemd –user - largely compatible with System V - the init.d scripts are supported and used as an alternative configuration - provides the /dev/initctl or /run/initctl interface (FIFO file for upstart commands) - support for the System V tools - how it works: systemd - loads the service configuration files from /etc/system/system or /lib/systemd/system (and/or /usr/lib/systemd/system) - determines the boot target, usually default.target - determines the boot target dependencies and activates them"},{"t":"Systemd targets","x":"Systemd targets - runlevel equivalents - poweroff.target – shutdown - rescue.target – single user mode - multi-user target – multi-user + networking - graphical.target – multi-user + networking + GUI - reboot.target – reboot - default-target – the default boot target, a symbolic link to one of the targets above - e.g.: booting into default-target - activates the dependent units: networking.service, crond.service, etc. - Note: boot targets are not the only ones controlled by systemd"},{"t":"Systemd units","x":"Systemd units - unit configuration files - encode information about services, devices, mount points, etc. - inspired by the XDG Desktop Entry Specification (like .desktop files) - contain several sections: [Unit], [Install] + specific sections (e.g. [Service] for service units) - [Unit] - unit description - controls the order and/or timing of activation - [Service] - this is where the services are started, stopped and reloaded - [Install] - used to express dependencies"},{"t":"Example unit file: ssh.service","x":"Example unit file: ssh.service [Unit] Description=OpenBSD Secure Shell server After=network.target auditd.service ConditionPathExists=!/etc/ssh/sshd_not_to_be_run [Service] EnvironmentFile=-/etc/default/ssh ExecStartPre=/usr/sbin/sshd –t ExecStart=/usr/sbin/sshd -D $SSHD_OPTS ExecReload=/usr/sbin/sshd –t ExecReload=/bin/kill -HUP $MAINPID KillMode=process Restart=on-failure RestartPreventExitStatus=255 Type=notify [Install] WantedBy=multi-user.target Alias=sshd.service"},{"t":"systemd commands","x":"systemd commands - implemented by systemctl - listing units $ systemctl list-units - starting/stopping/restarting units $ systemctl start ssh.service $ systemctl stop ssh.service $ systemctl restart ssh.service - enabling/disabling units $ systemctl enable ssh.service $ systemctl disable ssh.service"}]},{"id":"c8","pdf":"course8.pdf","kind":"lecture","n":8,"title":"Daemons and time management","blurb":"Daemonizing a program, syslog, scheduling with cron, anacron and at, and the hardware vs system clock.","topics":["daemons","syslogd","cron","crontab format","anacron","at","hwclock","date","time"],"ar":1.7778,"pages":[{"t":"Tools and Techniques in Computer Science","x":"Tools and Techniques in Computer Science Fall Semester 2026 Vlad Olaru"},{"t":"Lecture 8 - outline","x":"Lecture 8 - outline • daemons • time management cron anacron at"},{"t":"Daemons","x":"Daemons - processes (servers) that run in the background and are not associated with a controlling terminal - usually started as system services at the end of the boot process - servers started by a user from a shell (terminal) dissociate themselves from the terminal in order to avoid: - unwanted interactions with the job control performed by the shell - interaction with terminal session management - printing messages on the terminal (given that they run in the background) - examples - system services in /etc/rc.d started with superuser privileges (inetd/xinetd, Web, sendmail/postfix, syslogd etc.) - cron/at jobs invoked by the daemon cron/at - servers/programs started from a terminal"},{"t":"Turning programs into daemons","x":"Turning programs into daemons • (1) detaching from the terminal • (2) changing the current working directory the daemon changes its current working directory to / (or another directory of choice) core files generated by the daemon are saved in the current working directory if the daemon’s working directory were on a file system and the daemon continued to work there, that file system could not be unmounted (umount) • (3) closing all open file descriptors all file descriptors inherited from the process that created the daemon (normally, the shell) are closed • (4) redirecting stdin, stdout and stderr to /dev/null guarantees that the corresponding file descriptors are open, but reading returns EOF and writes are discarded necessary so that library functions that assume the use of these descriptors do not fail • (5) using syslogd for error logging"},{"t":"Syslogd","x":"Syslogd • without a controlling terminal, daemons need specific methods for displaying messages (e.g. error messages, emergencies, logging, etc.) • the standard method uses the syslog function, which sends messages to the daemon syslogd (rsyslogd on Linux systems) the configuration file syslog.conf, specifies what must be done with each message received by the daemon: append it to the end of a file log it on the console (/dev/console) forward it to the syslogd daemon of another machine the service runs on UDP port 514 (see /etc/services) on receiving the SIGHUP signal, the configuration file is re-read (modern systems use the service/systemctl reload commands) e.g.: $ kill –HUP <syslogd_pid> controlled by init/systemd through the previously discussed interfaces • messages can be sent directly to UDP port 514, but usually one uses the function syslog or the command logger"},{"t":"The syslog function","x":"The syslog function • in the absence of a controlling terminal, the daemon cannot use functions such as fprintf to stderr • the syslog function and the logger command, respectively, allow messages to be written to logs usually, files in /var/log or the console • logged messages are characterized by a combination of level and facility (level/facility) the combination is also called priority • the message level a value between 0 and 7 determines the importance of messages: emergency, alert, critical, error, warning, etc. allows all messages of a given level to be handled uniformly default value: LOG_NOTICE (level 5, normal but significant condition)"},{"t":"The syslog function (cont.)","x":"The syslog function (cont.) • facility specifies the type of program sending the message e.g.: kernel, authentication, time-related (cron/at), mail, printer messages, etc. default value: LOG_USER (generic user-level messages) the facility value allows all messages coming from a given facility to be handled in the same way in syslog.conf e.g.: kern.* /dev/console local7.debug /var/log/cisco.log"},{"t":"Using syslog","x":"Using syslog • viewing logged messages in real time $ tail –f /var/log/syslog • head/tail displays the beginning or the end of a file, respectively the output is usually specified in lines, with the option –n $ head –n 5 /etc/passwd • logging messages from the command line $ logger –p user.info Some user message of certain importance"},{"t":"Crond","x":"Crond • daemon that executes commands scheduled to run at a given time (usually periodically) • started as a system service at boot interactive, non-daemonized variant $ cron -f • loads into memory tables with the users’ task schedules, crontabs available in /var/spool/cron/crontabs/$USER created/edited with the command crontab /etc/crontab, the system table (with the variants /etc/cron.hourly, /etc/crond.daily, /etc/cron.weekly, /etc/cron.monthly)"},{"t":"How crond works","x":"How crond works • every 60 s it examines the contents of the crontabs to determine whether the respective commands must be run during the current minute • the output of the commands is mailed to the crontab owner or to the user specified in MAILTO • in addition, every minute it checks whether the spool directory or the system table have changed if so, it reloads the contents of all the crontabs crontab also implicitly updates the modification time of the spool directory"},{"t":"Crontab","x":"Crontab • command for managing user tables • in general, the tables are not edited directly • installing a crontab table $ crontab <file> # the file contains the schedule in a specific format • /etc/cron.allow and /etc/cron.deny can be used to control the permission to use a crontab format: one user name per line if they do not exist, depending on the system settings, either only root can use crond, or all users precedence: allow, deny"},{"t":"crontab operations","x":"crontab operations • editing launches $VISUAL or $EDITOR or /usr/bin/editor $ crontab -e • deleting $ crontab –r • displaying $ crontab –l"},{"t":"The crontab format","x":"The crontab format • spaces and newlines are ignored • # marks comments • two kinds of active lines environment variable settings commands • environment variable dereferencing does not work Ex: PATH=$HOME/bin:$PATH • recognized environment variables: SHELL=/bin/sh by default LOGNAME, HOME taken from /etc/passwd (LOGNAME cannot be changed) PATH=/usr/bin/:/bin , default value MAILTO, values: a user, a list of users; undefined implies $LOGNAME"},{"t":"The crontab format (cont.)","x":"The crontab format (cont.) • <min> <hour> <day> <month> <day of week> <cmd> “\\n” • the time fields can be: usual values, e.g.: min: 0-59, hours: 0-23, day: 1-31, month: 1-12, day of week: 0 – 7, mon-sun (0 and 7 are Sunday, Monday, Tuesday, etc.) ranges, e.g.: 8-11 for hours means hours 8,9,10,11 lists, e.g.: “1,2,5,9”, “0-4,8-12” steps, e.g.: “0-23/2” for hours, every 2 hours “*”: wildcard, meaning the entire possible range (e.g.: 0-23 for hours, 1-31 for days) • commands executed by /bin/sh or $SHELL “%” : the first occurrence is a newline; what follows goes to stdin “%” can be escaped with “\\”"},{"t":"crontab example","x":"crontab example # use /bin/bash to run commands, instead of /bin/sh SHELL=/bin/bash # the user to whom the output is mailed MAILTO=root # # run 5 minutes after midnight, every day 5 0 * * * $HOME/bin/daily.job >> $HOME/tmp/out 2>&1 # run at 2:15pm on the first day of every month 15 14 1 * * $HOME/bin/monthly # run at 10 pm on weekdays, annoying Joe 0 22 * * 1-5 mail -s \"It's 10pm\" joe%Joe,%%Where are your kids?% 23 0-23/2 * * * echo \"run 23 minutes after midnight, 2am, 4am ..., everyday\" 5 4 * * sun echo \"run at 5 after 4 every Sunday\" # run every second Saturday of the month 0 4 8-14 * * test $(date +\\%u) -eq 6 && echo \"2nd Saturday\" ‖ crontab example # use /bin/bash to run commands, instead of /bin/sh SHELL=/bin/bash # the user to whom the output is mailed MAILTO=root # # run 5 minutes after midnight, every day 50*** $HOME/bin/daily.job >> $HOME/tmp/out 2>&1 # run at 2:15pm on the first day of every month 15 14 1** $HOME/bin/monthly # run at 10 pm on weekdays, annoying Joe 0 22 * * 1-5 mail -s \"It's 10pm\" joe%Joe,%%Where are your kids?% 23 0-23/2 *** echo \"run 23 minutes after midnight, 2am, 4am ..., everyday\" 54** sun echo \"run at 5 after 4 every Sunday\" # run every second Saturday of the month 0 4 8-14 * * test $(date +\\%u) -eq 6 && echo \"2nd Saturday\""},{"t":"Anacron","x":"Anacron • cron requires the computer to run permanently • anacron runs commands periodically, with a frequency specified in days does not assume that the system runs continuously list of jobs in /etc/anacrontab line format: <period in days> <delay in min> <job id> <command> checks whether the command has been executed during the last period (the number of days above) if not, the command is executed after the specified delay when the command finishes, the date (without the time) is saved in a file in /var/spool/anacron for that job, so that it is known when to run it again the job id must match one of the command’s arguments the output of the jobs is mailed either to root or to $MAILTO, if it exists"},{"t":"At/atd","x":"At/atd • command used to control the deferred execution of commands • commands are placed in queues (designated by letters: a-z, A-Z) • at – schedules the execution of a command at a specified time • atq – lists the user’s jobs scheduled for later execution in a given queue (a is the default queue for at) • atrm – deletes a job scheduled for later execution • specifying the time HH:MM, MM/DD/YY, DD.MM.YY, etc. now + number of units E.g.: “4pm + 5 days”, “11am Jun 23”, “3pm tomorrow”, etc."},{"t":"at example","x":"at example $ at now + 1 minute warning: commands will be executed using /bin/sh at> ps auxw at> <EOT> job 4 at Mon Nov 25 20:07:00 2024 $ atq 4 Mon Nov 25 20:07:00 2024 a guest $ atrm 4 $ atq $"},{"t":"Time management, the HW clock","x":"Time management, the HW clock • two clocks: HW and system • hwclock command for accessing the system’s HW clock (RTC, CMOS) displays/sets the HW time $ hwclock --set --date sets the HW clock to the value of the system clock and the other way around $ hwclock --hctosys $ hwclock --systohc compensates for the drift of the HW clock (using the history in /etc/adjtime) $ hwclock --adjust compares the two clocks $ hwclock –c # compares periodically (10s) predicts future values of the HW clock based on the drift rate $ hwclock --predict etc."},{"t":"The system clock","x":"The system clock • a kernel component based on a timer (timer interrupt) • meaningful only while the computer is running • represents the number of seconds since 1 Jan 1970 00:00:00 • the clock that matters in the system, initialized at boot from the HW clock • date displays/sets the system clock uses formatting parameters introduced with “+” $ date +%d # displays the day of the month (e.g., 02) $ date +%m # the month $ date +%y # the year $ date +%H # the hour $ date +%M # the minute etc."},{"t":"Execution time of a command","x":"Execution time of a command • measuring the execution time of a command $ time find / -name crontab $ time sleep 5 • time is a shell built-in command, but there is also an external variant displays three times: the real time the time spent in user space the time spent in the kernel in addition, it summarizes the use of system resources a specific format is used in bash one uses the external command /usr/bin/time"}]},{"id":"c9","pdf":"course9.pdf","kind":"lecture","n":9,"title":"Data storage: block devices and file systems","blurb":"Disks and the memory hierarchy, block and loop devices, swap, fstab, journaling, tmpfs, NFS, procfs and sysfs.","topics":["storage hierarchy","block devices","loop devices","swap","inodes","fstab","journaling","tmpfs","NFS","procfs / sysfs"],"ar":1.7778,"pages":[{"t":"Tools and Techniques in Computer Science","x":"Tools and Techniques in Computer Science Fall Semester 2026 Vlad Olaru"},{"t":"Lecture 9 - outline","x":"Lecture 9 - outline • data storage block devices file systems"},{"t":"Data storage","x":"Data storage • main memory – the only large storage medium directly accessible to the processor random access usually volatile typically Dynamic Random-Access Memory (DRAM) • secondary storage – an extension of main memory that provides large capacity, non-volatile storage hard disks floppy disks CDROM/DVD SSD (Solid State Disks) flash drives etc."},{"t":"Data storage (cont.)","x":"Data storage (cont.) • hard disks (Hard Disk Drives, HDD) – rigid metal or glass platters covered with magnetic material capable of storing data the platters rotate around a spindle the surface of a platter is logically divided into tracks each track is in turn divided into sectors there are usually several platters => in 3D, the set of tracks at the same distance from the spindle forms a cylinder the disk controller (disk controller) determines the logical interaction between the device and the computer • non-volatile memory devices (Non-volatile memory, NVM)– faster than HDDs, non-volatile various technologies increasingly widespread"},{"t":"The memory hierarchy","x":"The memory hierarchy • storage systems are organized in hierarchies according to speed cost volatility • caching copies information into faster storage systems main memory is viewed as a cache for secondary storage • the device driver (Device Driver) a specialized kernel component; operates each device controller to manage I/O operations provides a uniform interface between the controller and the kernel"},{"t":"The storage device hierarchy","x":"The storage device hierarchy"},{"t":"Block devices in Unix","x":"Block devices in Unix - the data transfer unit = the data block - modern computer architectures based on Direct Memory Access (DMA) - data transfer between RAM and the block device without CPU mediation - HW support in the form of a specialized programmable chip - as opposed to the technique of programmed I/O - represented in Unix systems as block special files in the directory /dev E.g.: /dev/sda1, /dev/cdrom - characterized by a major number and a minor number - the first identifies the driver associated with the device, the second the number of the unit of that type in the system - created with mknod E.g.: $ mknod /dev/sdc1 b 8 1 ‖ Block devices in Unix - the data transfer unit = the data block - modern computer architectures based on Direct Memory Access (DMA) - data transfer between RAM and the block device without CPU mediation - HW support in the form of a specialized programmable chip - as opposed to the technique of programmed I/O - represented in Unix systems as block special files in the directory /dev E.g.: /dev/sda1, /dev/cdrom - characterized by a major number and a minor number - the first identifies the driver associated with the device, the second the number of the unit of that type in the system - created with mknod E.g.: $ mknod /dev/sdc1 b81"},{"t":"Loop devices","x":"Loop devices • a pseudo-device that allows an ordinary file (regular file) to be used as a block device • e.g.: a file system contained in a single file CDROM/DVD ISO images or floppy disk images can be mounted in the system with the mount command without HW support • usually /dev/loop0, /dev/loop1, etc. • losetup associates loop devices with ordinary files or block devices E.g.: $ losetup -f file.img # associates the first available loop device # with the file given as an argument $ losetup -j file.img # displays the status of the loop devices associated # with the image file given as argument"},{"t":"Using block devices","x":"Using block devices • data storage data recorded on disks according to a format specific to a given file system type usually, the format generates the logical access structure with files and collections of files (directories) writing the file system format to disk (“formatting the disk”) special commands are used for each file system type E.g.: $ mkfs –t ext4 /dev/sda1 #  $ mkfs.ext4 /dev/sda1 • swap space unformatted disks (in the sense above) used by the virtual memory of the operating system user programs can be larger than the available RAM insufficient RAM for new processes created with mkswap, enabled with swapon, disabled with swapoff E.g.: $ mkswap /dev/sda2 ; swapon /dev/sda2 ‖ Using block devices • data storage data recorded on disks according to a format specific to a given file system type usually, the format generates the logical access structure with files and collections of files (directories) writing the file system format to disk (“formatting the disk”) special commands are used for each file system type E.g.: $ mkfs –t ext4 /dev/sda1 #$ mkfs.ext4 /dev/sda1 • swap space unformatted disks (in the sense above) used by the virtual memory of the operating system user programs can be larger than the available RAM insufficient RAM for new processes created with mkswap, enabled with swapon, disabled with swapoff E.g.: $ mkswap /dev/sda2 ; swapon /dev/sda2"},{"t":"Useful commands","x":"Useful commands • parted/fdisk - manipulate the partition table of a disk $ parted –l /dev/sda # displays the partition table of /dev/sda • blkid/lsblk – provide information/attributes of block devices $ blkid; lsblk –f # disk attributes + FS information • df – reports information about disk space usage $ df –h # -h yields “human readable” output • du – estimates the space used by files $ du -sh * # total size of the files in the current directory • free – displays the available memory and swap space $ free –h # -h yields human readable figures"},{"t":"Files","x":"Files • file an operating system level abstraction for persistent data storage concretely, containers for persistent data storage at the lowest level, persistent storage is done on disks usually referred to by name (ASCII string), which the file system converts to an internal kernel representation called inode usual usage paradigm: open – read/write – close • besides data, files also store metadata (also part of the inode): attributes: date of last access, of last modification, file owner, permissions, file size, etc. the access structure for the low-level representation of the data (disk block addresses)"},{"t":"The file system","x":"The file system • a special OS component that manages files and directories • manages the persistent data storage medium structures the data on disk in a specific format • provides the user with a uniform data access interface based on the file abstraction and the operations (system calls) for working with files • modern OSs can integrate file systems with different formats in the same directory hierarchy VFS – Virtual Filesystem Switch (ext3, ext4, ntfs, vfat, etc) • made available to the user through the operation mount $ mount –t ext4 /dev/sda1 / $ umount /dev/sda1 # the reverse operation $ umount / # same as above • the directory in which the formatted disk is mounted is called a mountpoint"},{"t":"/etc/fstab","x":"/etc/fstab • system-wide table of mount points, inspected when the OS boots • at boot, the mount points in the table are mounted as if the following had been called $ mount -a • on Linux systems, in addition, systemd mounts the units of type mount • the mount points active at a given moment are available in /etc/mtab or /proc/mounts $ mount # displays the information in the tables • each mount point and its associated file system are described on one line of fstab each line contains 6 fields"},{"t":"The fstab fields","x":"The fstab fields • (1) fs_spec – the block device or remote file system to be mounted E.g.: /dev/sda7, /dev/cdrom, fmi.unibuc.ro:/home LABELS or UUIDs can be used – the preferred method, since device names can change when devices are added/removed $ blkid # or lsblk -f • (2) fs_file – the mount point (or none, for the swap partition) • (3) fs_vfstype – the file system type: ext4, xfs, vfat, ntfs, nfs, proc, etc. (swap denotes the swap file or partition) • (4) fs_mntops – the options of the mount operation for that file system defaults means rw, suid, dev, exec, auto, nouser, async user/owner, allows the mount operation for the user/owner noauto, the mount point is not mounted by the operation mount -a"},{"t":"The fstab fields (cont.)","x":"The fstab fields (cont.) • (5) fs_req – used for backups (dump), usually 0 • (6) fs_passno – used by fsck to determine the order of checks at boot the root / must have the value 1 the other mount points the value 2 default value 0 (fsck is not run at boot), usual for remote FSs Note: fsck checks (and repairs, if possible) a file system E.g.: $ fsck -t ext4 /dev/sdb2 $ fsck /dev/sda1 $ fsck /home Also, fsck can use LABELs/UUIDs"},{"t":"Types of file systems","x":"Types of file systems • file systems for permanently stored data log-structured/journaling or not • temporary file systems • file systems based on loop devices • distributed file systems • pseudo file systems"},{"t":"Log-structured/journaling FS","x":"Log-structured/journaling FS • updating the FS involves many writes • unwanted interruptions (system crash, power failure) in the middle of these writes can leave the internal data structures of the FS in an inconsistent state • detecting these inconsistencies and recovering from the inconsistent state requires an exhaustive analysis of the FS data structures usually, this involves running fsck done before the FS is mounted and used again for R/W if the FS is large and/or the I/O bandwidth is low => long downtime for the system • solution: log-structured/journaling FS allocates a special area (log/journal) that stores the changes that will later be applied to the FS after a crash, the log is read and the operations saved in the log are replayed until the internal FS data structures become consistent again"},{"t":"Example of an FS inconsistency","x":"Example of an FS inconsistency • file deletion scenario the user writes a program that opens a file the program runs for a long time without closing the file before the program ends, the user deletes the file if it was the last link, the kernel tries to delete the corresponding inode from the link’s directory entry because the program still has an open reference to the file, the inode is not deleted from the FS (it will actually be deleted only when the program closes the file) the power goes off => the current state of the FS on the HDD is the following the directory from which the user deleted the file no longer contains any entry (link, inode) the FS (on disk) still contains the inode of the file (link) deleted by the user when the computer restarts, fsck is run (possibly from /etc/fstab) => the program detects the inconsistency, recovers the deleted file into a special directory, lost+found and deletes the file (the inode) from the disk"},{"t":"Log-structured/journaling FS (cont.)","x":"Log-structured/journaling FS (cont.) • concretely: the FS records each metadata update as a transaction • all transactions are written to a log/journal a transaction is committed as soon as it has been written (sequentially) to the log sometimes a separate device or a dedicated section of the disk is used in any case, at this point the FS may not yet have been updated • the transactions in the log are written asynchronously to the FS data structures once such a structure has been modified, the transaction is removed from the log • on a crash, all the transactions remaining in the log must be executed again • consequences: (1) fast crash recovery (2) eliminates the possibility of metadata inconsistencies • E.g: ext4, xfs, reiserfs, etc."},{"t":"Temporary FS (tmpfs)","x":"Temporary FS (tmpfs) • a volatile file system, stored in RAM the data stored in tmpfs is lost after the FS is unmounted (umount), after a reboot or on a power failure • it is not a RAM disk; it has the logical structure of an FS, with high-level abstractions such as files and directories RAM disk – a virtual disk, organized according to HDD principles an FS could possibly run atop of a RAM disk • benefits from the swap space! solves the problem of allocation limits (out-of-memory) • VFS type: tmpfs E.g: $ mount –t tmpfs ramfs /mnt –o size=1g • Note: there is no device in the command; it is replaced by a string chosen by the user (i.e., ramfs) • usefulness: storing temporary data (server configuration data, FIFOs for IPC between system programs, etc.), special file systems such as cgroups, etc."},{"t":"Loop device FS","x":"Loop device FS • by associating a file with a loop device, the contents of the file can be used with the command mount • E.g.: CDROM mount $ mount -o loop -t iso9660 cdrom.iso /cdrom • E.g.: swap space that uses a file instead of a device $ dd if=/dev/zero of=~/myswapfile bs=1K count=1M $ losetup --show -f ~/myswapfile # assume the chosen device is /dev/loop0 $ mkswap /dev/loop0 $ swapon /dev/loop0 $ swapon"},{"t":"Loop device FS (cont.)","x":"Loop device FS (cont.) • E.g.: creating a file system inside a file $ dd if=/dev/zero of=~/ext4.img bs=1K count=1M $ losetup --show -f ~/ext4.img # assume the chosen device is /dev/loop0 $ mkfs.ext4 /dev/loop0 # creates the ext4 format $ file ~/ext4.img $ mount /dev/loop0 /mnt … $ umount/dev/loop0 $ losetup -d /dev/loop0"},{"t":"Parallel/distributed file systems","x":"Parallel/distributed file systems • message passing systems, client-server paradigm • FS server remote program that exports (part of) the local FS to remote machines usually a directory on the remote machine is exported • FS client client program that serves as an intermediary between the FS server and the local VFS • Ex: Network File System (NFS) based on Remote Procedure Calls (RPC); executes remote procedures as if they were local the local procedure (e.g., read) is executed by a stub client that contacts the FS server to execute the actual procedure remotely (stub server) the call parameters and the operation result are converted between the local data format and the network format (XDR, ASN.1) and back (marshalling/unmarshalling operations) $ mount –t nfs fmi.unibuc.ro:/home /home"},{"t":"Pseudo file systems","x":"Pseudo file systems • in general, interfaces to the kernel data structures • procfs $ mount –t proc proc /proc • mainly contains a subdirectory for each process in the system, of the form /proc/[pid] • a [pid] subdirectory contains information about the process, such as the command line used to launch the process the process’s cpuset cwd, environment, executable link, open file descriptors (including 0,1,2) statistics on the process’s I/O operations access to the process’s memory pages + the process’s pagemap the file systems mounted in the process’s namespace the process state used by the command ps information about memory usage (size, resident size, text, data, lib) description of the current system call (arguments, SP, PC) information about the process’s threads and timers etc."},{"t":"Pseudo file systems (proc)","x":"Pseudo file systems (proc) • in addition, /proc contains information about PCI devices ( /proc/bus/pci/devices, visible with the lspci command) the kernel command line at boot (/proc/cmdline) the CPUs and their architecture (/proc/cpuinfo) the list of device major numbers ( /proc/devices ) the list of file systems supported by the kernel ( /proc/filesystems ) the system load average displayed by the uptime command ( /proc/loadavg ) memory usage statistics displayed by the free command ( /proc/meminfo ) the list of modules loaded in the system ( /proc/modules ) the list of file systems currently mounted in the system ( /proc/mounts, see mount) the system’s network, subdirectory /proc/net the list of disk partitions in the system ( /proc/partitions ) kernel statistics ( /proc/stat ) the swap partitions currently in use ( /proc/swaps ) the values of various kernel variables ( /proc/sys ) statistics about the virtual memory system ( /proc/vmstat ), etc."},{"t":"Pseudo file systems (sysfs)","x":"Pseudo file systems (sysfs) • sysfs $ mount –t sysfs sysfs /sys • exports kernel information about devices, kernel modules, file systems, etc. • subdirectories /sys/block – symbolic links to /sys/devices for each device in the system /sys/devices – the tree of kernel data structures for devices /sys/fs – contains subdirectories for special file systems (e.g., cgroups) /sys/kernel/ - contains files and directories with information about the kernel state /sys/kernel/mm – contains files and directories with information about the memory management system /sys/module – contains information about each kernel module in the system etc."}]},{"id":"c10","pdf":"course10.pdf","kind":"lecture","n":10,"title":"Compiling, linking, debugging and make","blurb":"From source to executable, static and dynamic libraries, compiler options, gdb and Makefiles.","topics":["compiler & linker","static vs dynamic","libraries","cc options","gdb","Makefile"],"ar":1.7778,"pages":[{"t":"Tools and Techniques in Computer Science","x":"Tools and Techniques in Computer Science Fall Semester 2026 Vlad Olaru"},{"t":"Lecture 10 - outline","x":"Lecture 10 - outline • compiling and linking programs • debugging programs • managing programming projects"},{"t":"From source to executable","x":"From source to executable • source – file written in a programming language (e.g. C, C++) • compiler – translates the source into a low-level language (machine code) • object – binary file resulting from compiling a source file • library – (already compiled) binary file providing some functionality (e.g. networking functions) • linker – links objects and libraries to produce the executable • static executable – all objects and libraries are included in the resulting executable • dynamic executable – contains only its own data and code + stubs for library calls; libraries are kept in separate files"},{"t":"Load time","x":"Load time Resolving the dependencies of dynamic executables when they are loaded into memory: 1. the operating system loads the executable into memory 2. before it is executed, the libraries it uses are looked up 3. all the necessary libraries are loaded 4. if one or more libraries are not found, the executable is removed from memory and the execution is aborted"},{"t":"Run time","x":"Run time Resolving the dependencies of dynamic executables on demand: 1. the operating system loads the executable into memory 2. before it is executed, only the libraries strictly necessary for launching the program are looked up 3. during execution, if a library function is needed 1. the program’s execution is stopped 2. the required library is looked up in the system 3. it is loaded into memory 4. the program’s execution resumes where it left off 4. at any moment, if a library is not found and loaded, the executable is removed from memory and the execution is aborted"},{"t":"Compiling","x":"Compiling http://codex.cs.yale.edu/avi/os-book/"},{"t":"Compilers","x":"Compilers UNIX solutions: • gcc – GNU Compiler Collection (C, C++, Java, etc.) • clang – LLVM front-end for C, C++, Objective C, OpenCL, etc. Regardless of the compiler, UNIX provides the command cc (C compiler) and, optionally, the command c++ (C++ compiler)."},{"t":"A simple executable","x":"A simple executable In its simplest form, the compiler • takes the source files as arguments • produces the executable a.out • historical name: the standard executable format on the first version of UNIX $ l s hello . c $ cc hello . c $ ls a. out hello . c $ ./ a. out Hello, World ! Execution prefixed with . / - the shell looks for the executable in the current directory (not in $PATH) With an executable name: $ cc –o hello hello.c $ ./hello ‖ A simple executable In its simplest form, the compiler • takes the source files as arguments • produces the executable a.out • historical name: the standard executable format on the first version of UNIX $ls hello .c$ cc hello .c$ ls a. out hello .c$ ./ a. out Hello, World ! Execution prefixed with ./- the shell looks for the executable in the current directory (not in $PATH) With an executable name: $ cc –o hello hello.c $ ./hello"},{"t":"Objects","x":"Objects • generated with the option -c • the compiler stops after producing the object • it does not go on to create an executable • the object will be used together with other objects and libraries to produce the final executable $ l s hello . c $ cc −c hello . c $ l s hello .o hello . c ‖ Objects • generated with the option -c • the compiler stops after producing the object • it does not go on to create an executable • the object will be used together with other objects and libraries to produce the final executable $ls hello .c$ cc −c hello .c$ls hello .o hello . c"},{"t":"Predefined libraries","x":"Predefined libraries • External libraries are accessed with the option - lname (no spaces!) Note: the full name of the library is libname.a or libname.so • the compiler searches for the library in a list of directories • the list is usually generated and maintained by the command ldconfig(1) • the environment variable $LD_LIBRARY_PATH contains user-defined directories that hold libraries • the library found is used together with other objects and libraries to produce the final executable Using the math function library libm.a or libm.so: $ cc h e l l o . c − l m ‖ Predefined libraries • External libraries are accessed with the option - lname (no spaces!) Note: the full name of the library is libname.a or libname.so • the compiler searches for the library in a list of directories • the list is usually generated and maintained by the command ldconfig(1) • the environment variable $LD_LIBRARY_PATH contains user-defined directories that hold libraries • the library found is used together with other objects and libraries to produce the final executable Using the math function library libm.a or libm.so: $ cc hello.c−lm"},{"t":"Creating static libraries","x":"Creating static libraries • an archive is created with the object files $ ar –r libhello.a hello.o • optionally, the archive is checked $ ar –t libhello.a $ objdump –p libhello.a $ nm libhello.a • the program is linked $ gcc –o main main.c –L. -lhello • the program is run $ ./main"},{"t":"Creating dynamic libraries","x":"Creating dynamic libraries • the object files are created in fpic/fPIC format $ gcc –c hello.c -fpic • the dynamic library (shared object) is created $ gcc –shared –o libhello.so hello.o • optionally, the library is placed in the system’s predefined directories (/lib,/usr/lib,/usr/local/lib, etc.) and ldconfig is run • the program is linked (with –L if the previous step was not performed) $ gcc –o main main.c –L. -lhello • the program’s dependencies are checked $ ldd main"},{"t":"Running dynamically linked programs","x":"Running dynamically linked programs • if the library is in the rpath (ldconfig has been run, ldd finds it) $ ./main • otherwise, the path to the dynamic library must be specified $ LD_LIBRARY_PATH=. ./main or $ export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=. $ ./main"},{"t":"Useful compilation options","x":"Useful compilation options Useful options frequently used when compiling • -o name – the name of the resulting executable or object (e.g. hello instead of a.out) • -Wall – displays all warning messages • -g – prepares the executable for a debugging session (debug) • -Onumber – the optimization level (0 →no optimizations) • -O2 is used by default, -O0 is often used together with -g for debugging E.g.: compiling hello.c with debug symbols, without optimization, into the executable named hello $ cc −g −O0 hello . c −o hello"},{"t":"Debugging","x":"Debugging Notions used in a debugging session • break, breakpoint • point at which the execution should stop • it can be a function, a line in a source file, or even an assembly instruction • the state of the variables at that moment can be inspected • backtrace, trace, stack trace • the chain of function calls that led to the current point • example: main() →decompress() →zip decompress() • watch, watchpoint • point at which the execution should stop if a user-specified condition is met • it can be the modification of a memory area or of a variable The most common program used for debugging executables is gdb(1)."},{"t":"Commands in gdb(1)","x":"Commands in gdb(1) Common commands • break symbol – sets a breakpoint at that symbol (e.g. main() or hello.c:10) • run – starts the execution (usually after the breakpoints have been set) • next – once a stopping point is reached, go to the next instruction • continue – continue to the next stopping point or until the program terminates • print variable – displays the value of a variable • quit – exit from gdb(1)"},{"t":"Debugging session","x":"Debugging session hel l o f i l e hello . c , l i ne 5. $ cc −g −O0 hel l o . c −o $ gdb hello (gdb) break main Br eakpoint 1 at 0x546 : ( gdb) run St art ing program : /home/user/src/hel l o Br eakpoint 1 at 0x101a49c00546 : f i l e hel l o . c , l i ne 5. 1, main () at hel lo . c :5 pri nt f (” Hello , World !” ) ; Br eakpoint 5 ( gdb) next return 0; Hello , World ! 6 ( gdb) continue Co ntinuing . Program exited normally . ( gdb) quit ‖ Debugging session hel lofile hello .c,li ne 5. $ cc −g −O0 hel lo.c −o $ gdb hello (gdb) break main Br eakpoint 1 at 0x546 : ( gdb) run St art ing program : /home/user/src/hel l o Br eakpoint 1 at 0x101a49c00546 :file hel lo.c,li ne 5. 1, main () at hel lo . c :5 pri nt f (” Hello , World !” ) ; Br eakpoint 5 ( gdb) next return 0; Hello , World !6( gdb) continue Co ntinuing . Program exited normally . ( gdb) quit"},{"t":"Projects","x":"Projects Software products consist of several components: • different modules (e.g. communication, logging) • different libraries (e.g. compression, cryptography, http) • several source files (also called compilation units) • there are various dependencies between them (e.g. the communication module depends on the http library) • the inter-dependencies dictate the compilation order • the dependencies and the compilation order are formally described in files of type Makefile • the instructions in a Makefile are executed with the command make(1) Note: Makefile is the default name, but any other name can be used $ make –f MyOwnMakefile"},{"t":"Makefile","x":"Makefile Fixed format t a r g e t : dependency1 dependency2 [. . . ] commands • target – the result of the rule • dependency – the required resources (already existing) • commands – the commands for producing the target • Attention! – there is a TAB before commands • the first target in the file or, if it exists, the target a l l will be executed by default (i.e., calling make without a parameter) Advantage: only the modified files are recompiled! ‖ Makefile Fixed format target: dependency1 dependency2 [. ..] commands • target – the result of the rule • dependency – the required resources (already existing) • commands – the commands for producing the target • Attention! – there is a TAB before commands • the first target in the file or, if it exists, the target all will be executed by default (i.e., calling make without a parameter) Advantage: only the modified files are recompiled!"},{"t":"A simple Makefile","x":"A simple Makefile hello . c $ cat M akefile al l : hell o hello : hello . c cc −o hell o clean : rm hello $ make cc −o hell o hello . c $ make clean rm hello $ make hello cc −o hell o hello . c 20 # make all ‖ A simple Makefile hello .c$ cat M akefile al l : hell o hello : hello . c cc −o hell o clean : rm hello $ make cc −o hell o hello .c$ make clean rm hello $ make hello cc −o hell o hello . c 20 # make all"},{"t":"Makefile variables","x":"Makefile variables Useful variables • $ @ – the name of the current target (left-hand side) • $^ – all the dependencies (right-hand side) • $< – the name of the first dependency (e.g. dependency1) • .type1.type2: – the target transforms files of type 1 into files of type 2 (e.g. . c . o : ) • $(VAR:old=new) – replaces old with new in variable VAR ‖ Makefile variables Useful variables •$@– the name of the current target (left-hand side) • $^ – all the dependencies (right-hand side) • $< – the name of the first dependency (e.g. dependency1) • .type1.type2: – the target transforms files of type 1 into files of type 2 (e.g. .c.o:)• $(VAR:old=new) – replaces old with new in variable VAR"},{"t":"A complex Makefile","x":"A complex Makefile PROG=h el l o SRCS=h el l o . c OBJS=$ (SRCS : . c=.o) CC=cc CFLAGS=−g −O0 INSTALL=i n s t a l l al l : $(PROG) $(PROG) : $(OBJS) $(CC) −o $@ $ˆ $(CFLAGS) . c .o: $(CC) −c −o $@ $< $(CFLAGS) i n st a l l : $(PROG) $(INSTALL) $< ${HOME}/ bin clean : −rm $(PROG) $(OBJS) ‖ A complex Makefile PROG=h el l o SRCS=h el lo.c OBJS=$ (SRCS : . c=.o) CC=cc CFLAGS=−g −O0 INSTALL=i nstall al l : $(PROG) $(PROG) : $(OBJS) $(CC) −o $@ $ˆ $(CFLAGS) . c .o: $(CC) −c −o $@ $< $(CFLAGS) i n st all: $(PROG) $(INSTALL) $< ${HOME}/ bin clean : −rm $(PROG) $(OBJS)"}]},{"id":"c11","pdf":"course11.pdf","kind":"lecture","n":11,"title":"Networking","blurb":"Networks and protocols, OSI vs internet model, IP, TCP, UDP, DNS, sockets, HTTP and email.","topics":["protocols","OSI model","IP","TCP / UDP","DNS","sockets","HTTP","email"],"ar":1.7778,"pages":[{"t":"Tools and Techniques in Computer Science","x":"Tools and Techniques in Computer Science Fall Semester 2026 Vlad Olaru"},{"t":"Lecture 11 - outline","x":"Lecture 11 - outline • networking • concepts network protocol sockets • protocol examples"},{"t":"Context","x":"Context - inter-process communication (IPC) services - pipes/FIFOs $ ls –l | more - sockets: Unix or TCP/IP - paradigm difference: shared memory vs message passing - pipe – builds a communication channel in the (shared) kernel memory of the same machine => consequences: the synchronization of the processes that write to/read from the pipe is ensured by the kernel, as is the FIFO data access policy - socket TCP/IP– builds a communication channel between two different machines connected to each other by a communication network => consequences: the complexity of operating the communication network (which may involve several intermediate machines in the message exchange) requires the use of communication protocols"},{"t":"Network","x":"Network Intranet or network • made up of nodes (computers) called hosts • physical links: network cables, wireless channels • logical links: the path, through the physical links, between a source node and a destination node • problems: graph traversal, shortest path, travelling salesman, network flows • a network can be connected to one or more networks Internet: a set of interconnected public networks"},{"t":"Communication protocol","x":"Communication protocol Network communication follows one or more protocols • described in Request for Comments documents (RFC) • Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) • Internet Society (ISOC) • committees made up of engineers and computer scientists • https://www.rfc-editor.org/retrieve/ • Note: a protocol is a specification, not an implementation"},{"t":"Communication protocols","x":"Communication protocols • the OSI model (Open Systems Interconnection) 7 layers: physical, data link, network, transport, session, presentation, application • alternative model, the internet protocols (a.k.a. the Department of Defense, DoD, protocols): combines the last three OSI layers into a single layer: application combines the physical and data link layers into a single layer: link • conceptually, it works on the protocol stack principle: the highest-level protocol (application) calls on the services of the protocol immediately below it (presentation), and so on at the lowest level, the data sent by the data link protocols is sent over the physical network on reception, the reverse path is followed • socket : abstracts the communication performed at the application level using the internet transport-level protocols"},{"t":"Communication protocols examples","x":"Communication protocols examples • data link (LAN networks): Ethernet (CSMA/CD), Token Ring, ATM, Wireless • network: IP • data transport: TCP, UDP, SCTP • session: RPC • data presentation: XDR, ASN.1, encryption, compression • application: DNS, SMTP, HTTP, FTP, SSH, etc."},{"t":"Internet Protocol (IP)","x":"Internet Protocol (IP) • each host is identified by one or more IP addresses • the initial version (IPv4) uses 32 bits (RFC791) • the current version uses 128 bits (IPv6) • IPv4 is still the most widely used • the 32 bits represent 4 bytes, grouped and separated by . • range: 0.0.0.0 – 255.255.255.255 • certain sub-ranges are used for private addressing (non-routable addresses) • the remaining public addresses are accessible from any node in the network • e.g.: 192.168.1.1, 10.0.0.1, 216.58.214.238, 8.8.8.8"},{"t":"Transport Control Protocol (TCP)","x":"Transport Control Protocol (TCP) • assembles/disassembles messages into packets transmitted over heterogeneous communication channels (TCP) • ensures in-order delivery of packets at the destination (TCP) • automatically solves packet routing problems over alternative communication paths between the source and destination nodes when certain intermediate machines are unavailable (IP) • ensures automatic retransmission of lost/corrupted packets (TCP) • controls communication in the presence of network congestion (TCP) • etc."},{"t":"Domain Name System (DNS)","x":"Domain Name System (DNS) The name associated with an IP address • easier for human users to remember • protocol described in RFC1035 • if an IP address changes, the name stays the same • initially, everyone kept a local address book in /etc/hosts • today the process is centralized • everyone uses the same address book • several hosts, called name servers, which hold a copy of the address book • the main servers from which the others copy the information are called root name servers • e.g.: fmi.unibuc.ro →193.226.51.6 • Unix commands: nslookup(1), whois(1), dig(1)"},{"t":"Distributed system architecture","x":"http://abcnetworking.wikispaces.com Distributed system architecture"},{"t":"Socket","x":"Socket Client-server communication is done through sockets • an operating system level abstraction • address: the IP address of a host • port: an entry point of the host • server port: identifies a service (see /etc/services for well-known ports) • client port: identifies the client process • the address and the port form an endpoint • socket: defines an endpoint • a connection is made between 2 endpoints using a common protocol • one socket for the server and one for the client • e.g.: <TCP,192.168.1.6:4444,193.226.51.6:80>"},{"t":"TCP sockets","x":"TCP sockets • communication channel (a connection) between two endpoints (IP addr, port) • guarantees correct and in-order delivery of messages between source and destination • lost messages are retransmitted automatically • the communication channel has byte-stream semantics (similar to a pipe) • therefore, bytes are read from a TCP socket, not whole messages!"},{"t":"UDP sockets","x":"UDP sockets • communication channel without guarantees between two endpoints • messages (called datagrams in this case, as in the IP protocol) can be lost, are not retransmitted automatically, and are not delivered in order • messages are read whole, i.e. one datagram at a time (unlike TCP)"},{"t":"Examples of simple application-level communication protocols","x":"Examples of simple application-level communication protocols • daytime, echo, time • client: emulated with telnet-type commands $ telnet host port • server: standard daemons accessible through the Internet superserver (inetd/xinetd) server with a particular structure that multiplexes several different services the multiplexed services are configured in configuration files located in /etc/xinetd.d • in general, servers have configuration files, while clients only have call parameters on the command line the difference is dictated by the different complexity of the two types of programs • services are controlled with the command service/systemctl $ service <service> <start|stop|reload>"},{"t":"www World Wide Web ( )","x":"World Wide Web (www) • created at CERN and published in 1991 • resources identified by Uniform Resource Locators (URL) • web pages presented as hypertext • HyperText Transfer Protocol (HTTP) RFC2616 • serve web pages on port 80 • encrypted access through HTTPS on port 443 (soon the default) • e.g.: • http://fmi.unibuc.ro:80, • http://fmi.unibuc.ro • http://fmi.unibuc.ro:443 • https://fmi.unibuc.ro:443 • https://fmi.unibuc.ro • Unix browser: lynx(1), w3m(1) • Unix crawler: curl(1), wget(1)"},{"t":"HTTP communication example","x":"HTTP communication example • telnet <host> 80 • then the command GET / HTTP/1.1 (or GET /index.html HTTP/1.1) Host: <host> <cr/lf> <cr/lf> • telnet fmi.unibuc.ro 80"},{"t":"Email – IMAP, POP","x":"Email – IMAP, POP • mail access • Post Office Protocol (POP) RFC1939 • POP makes a local copy of the messages and deletes them from the mail server • Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) RFC3501 • IMAP operates on the messages directly on the server • IMAP can keep a (partial) local copy • ports: POP 110, POP3S 995, IMAP 143, IMAPS 993 • the mailbox is kept in the format mbox or maildir • mbox: keeps all messages in a single file • maildir: keeps each message in a separate file"},{"t":"Email – SMTP","x":"Email – SMTP Sending messages • Simple Message Transfer Protocol (SMTP) RFC821 • SMTP sends a message to a recipient • old protocol, does not require a sender →spam, spoofing • old protocol, does not require authentication →spam, spoofing • ports: SMTP 25, SMTPS • anti-spam solutions • reverse DNS: IP link →name • authentication: user and password, encryption • Sender Policy Framework (SPF), DKIM (DomainKeys Identified Mail), DMARC (Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting and Conformance): anti-email spoofing • databases of spammer IPs"}]},{"id":"projects","pdf":"project-list.pdf","kind":"info","title":"Project list","blurb":"Project rules and the 14 project topics (bash / awk only).","topics":["rules","14 topics"],"ar":0.7071,"pages":[{"t":"List of projects","x":"List of projects 1 Regulation • The project must be completed by the end of the semester • The project is worth a maximum of 35 points • All team members receive the same score • Each member must present their contribution (aﬀecting the ﬁnal grade accordingly) • Programming is to be done exclusively in bash or awk. 2 Projects 1. LazyWGET (2 students) – Write a shell script lwget that uses the wget command NON- RECURISIVELY (i.e. you cannot use the command with the -r ﬂag) to download HTML documents from the internet to your local computer. lwget downloads a SINGLE HTML ﬁle from the internet using the wget command at a time, parses its content, and then recursively downloads the HTML resources from the document, emulating the behavior of the wget -r command. The diﬀerence from wget -r is that the recursive download of resources referenced in the HTML document is done lazily, meaning that all HTML tags that deﬁne resources to be downloaded are deﬁned as promises that are not evaluated until the lwget command forces these promises through a new call. Speciﬁcally, each lwget <URL> command forces the evaluation of the current level of recur- sion in document trees: the ﬁrst call downloads the HTML ﬁle provided as a command-line parameter (the root of the document tree), the second call parses this ﬁle and downloads lo- cally the documents referenced by the HTML ﬁle, the third call parses all HTML documents downloaded in the previous step and downloads the HTML documents referenced by them, and so on. After each step, you can use usual ls or tree commands to verify the behavior of the lwget command. Write a graphical interface to lwget (e.g., based on libraries like ncurses and/or utilities like dialog or whiptail) that helps the user navigate through the HTML hierarchy and force the evaluation of the current level of recursion. 2. FIFO-Manpage-Server (2–3 students) – Write client-server programs based on bash scripts that use special FIFO ﬁles as communication channels between clients and the server. The server uses a well-known FIFO ﬁle, conﬁgurable in a conﬁguration ﬁle. The server receives"},{"t":"requests from clients in the FIFO ﬁle for manual pages related to various commands. The fo","x":"requests from clients in the FIFO ﬁle for manual pages related to various commands. The format of the client requests is as follows: BEGIN-REQ [client-pid: command-name] END-REQ where client-pid is the client’s process ID and command-name is the name of the shell com- mand for which the client is requesting the corresponding information from the man page (i.e., executing the man <command-name> command). The server reads the requests from the FIFO and replies to the clients in FIFO ﬁles customized for each client. These customized FIFO ﬁles have a standard name that contains the client’s PID (e.g. /tmp/server-reply-XXXX where XXXX is the value of the client’s PID). The actual reply is the contents of the man pages for command-name. After reading the man pages and displaying their contents on the screen, the client deletes its personal FIFO ﬁle. The client uses a a graphical interface (e.g., based on libraries like ncurses and/or utilities like dialog or whiptail) to display the man page on the screen. The user interface should have a top menu bar listing some of the most important sections of the manpage (NAME, SYNOPSIS, DESCRIPTION, etc) which must be highlighted with a special color and act like links in a browser, that is to say, when the user selects them, the pager jumps to that section. 3. FIFO-RSHD (2–3 students) – Write shell programs (scripts) for \"remote shell\" clients and servers that use special FIFO ﬁles as communication channels between clients and servers. The main (master) RSHD server uses a well-known FIFO ﬁle, conﬁgurable in a conﬁguration ﬁle. The server receives requests from clients in the FIFO ﬁle for the execution of ”remote” commands. The format of the client requests is as follows: BEGIN-REQ [client-pid: command] END-REQ For example: BEGIN-REQ [client-pid: ls -l wc -l] END-REQ where client-pid is the client’s process ID and command is the name of the shell command that the client requests the server to execute. The master server reads the requests from the FIFO and sends them through N FIFO ﬁles in Round-Robin mode (circular, modulo N) to N slave servers that are responsible for the actual execution of the client commands. Each slave server that executes a client command will respond to the client by redirecting the standard output of the executed command to FIFO ﬁles customized for each client. These customized FIFO ﬁles have a standard name that contains the client’s PID (e.g. /tmp/server-reply-XXXX where XXXX represents the client’s PID value taken from the client’s request). The client reads from the FIFO ﬁle that corresponds to it (which has its PID in the name) the result of the command execution and displays it on the screen. After reading the results and displaying them on the screen, the client deletes its personal FIFO ﬁle. 4. Watchdog (2 students) – Write a shell script that uses the top command in single-frame batch mode to keep track of a list of running processes and periodically monitor their re- sources. The program should not allow resource consumption beyond thresholds speciﬁed individually per process and per resource (i.e., process K should not consume more than 5% of CPU time and should not have more than 1GB of RAM resident at any given time). The program also ensures that processes in the list are restarted if they unexpectedly exit. The restriction can be imposed either permanently, by killing the process that has exceeded its resource usage quota, or temporarily, by suspending the process and resuming it when the"},{"t":"general conditions of the system allow the process to resume and increase its quota (let’s","x":"general conditions of the system allow the process to resume and increase its quota (let’s say memory has been freed and you can increase the process’s resident memory quota). Examples of monitored resources: time since the process started (cumulative or not), proces- sor idle time, time spent in user-space/kernel, process resident RAM, shared memory used by the process, total non-swapped physical memory of the process, virtual memory used by the process, swap memory used by the process, number of page faults, number of threads of the process, etc. Whichever of these examples you choose to monitor, explain the reason why you chose to impose a threshold on the use of that resource. Write a graphical interface (e.g., based on libraries like ncurses and/or utilities like dialog or whiptail) that helps the user vizualize in a comfortable way the information displayed by the program. 5. MySort (2 students) – Write a shell program (script) that implements the Shell sort al- gorithm used by the Unix sort command. Then write a new version of the program that uses Bitonic sort. Test both versions of the program against the performance of the sort command. Write a graphical interface (e.g., based on libraries like ncurses and/or utilities like dialog or whiptail) that helps steer the program, that is to say, helps the user input the data (from the keyboard or from a ﬁle), start a given sorting algorithm, show and compare the results of the algorithms. N.B. The sort command is more complex, the programs you write will only sort numbers, that is, they will emulate the behavior of the sort -n command. 6. SLCheck (2 students) – Write a shell program (script) that receives as a parameter a directory (possibly even the root of the ﬁle system) and checks for broken links, symbolic links that refer to non-existent ﬁles. The program works recursively, checking the entire hierarchy of ﬁles and directories accessible starting from the directory provided as a parameter to the program. When it encounters a symbolic link to a directory, the program’s default behavior is not to dereference the link, that is, not to recursively start the analysis of the referenced directory. This behavior can be changed if the program is called with the –follow-symlinks ﬂag, in which case the recursive analysis continues with the directory referenced in the symbolic link. Write a graphical interface (e.g., based on libraries like ncurses and/or utilities like dialog or whiptail) that helps the user navigate the directory hierarchy, identify symbolic links and, if desired, follow them, and mark visually with a diﬀerent color the broken links. Tip: The easiest way to generate broken links is to use a tmpfs ﬁle system on which to copy a directory structure, for example /etc. When uninstalling the ﬁle system (umount operation), the data automatically disappears and any symbolic links created to the RAM disk become broken. N.B. The deep traversal of the directory hierarchy must be implemented directly in the program, and the use of shell commands that help with this task, such as the find command, is not allowed. 7. PackageMonitor (2–3 students) – Write a shell program (script) that monitors existing software packages on a Linux system using information from /var/log/dpkg.log. The pro- gram mainly monitors the install and remove operations. The program has two main com- ponents: a user front-end and the monitor itself. The monitor is launched periodically from"},{"t":"cron and parses the information from /var/log/dpkg.log which it organizes in a working dir","x":"cron and parses the information from /var/log/dpkg.log which it organizes in a working directory in such a way as to allow indexing by the name of the software package, respectively by date. Each software package has its own subdirectory in the working directory where the history of the package’s install/remove operations is found. The front-end analyzes the in- formation collected by the monitor in the working directory and oﬀers the user the following functionalities: - list of software packages currently installed in the system and the date of the last installation - list of software packages that were once installed in the system and were removed in the meantime as well as the date of the last removal - history of operations performed over time on a speciﬁc software package - list of software packages installed/removed in a given period of time The monitor analysis can be done over a ﬂexible period of time, provided as a parameter in the command line. Additionaly: - the program detects partially installed packages and determines whether they were ﬁnally installed correctly - the program detects if a package has been installed for the ﬁrst time on the system - the front-end prints the installed size of an installed package on request - the program maintains the sum of the installed sizes of the packages installed on the system and updates it accordingly with each install/remove - the program implements an undo cache that retains the most recent N removed packages and displays them on request, so that they can be reinstalled if necessary. The cache is managed (LRU Least Recently Used), meaning the oldest entry is removed from the cache if there is no more space in the cache and a new package has just been removed from the system and needs to be put in the undo cache. Write a graphical interface (e.g., based on libraries like ncurses and/or utilities like dialog or whiptail) that helps vizualize the information produced by the program. 8. PackageMonitorInotify (2–3 students) – Write a shell program (script) that implements the PackageMonitor functionality above using the Linux inotify interface to monitor changes to the /var/log/dpkg.log ﬁle. N.B. Use the commands that implement the functionality of the inotify interface, not the programming API (ie, write a shell program, not a C program). 9. AutomounterShell (2 students) – Write an amsh shell program (script) that implements a shell. The program displays a prompt (eg, amsh>), reads external commands entered from the keyboard in a loop and executes them using the sh -c command. To navigate through the ﬁle system, amsh must implement the internal cd command, which has a diﬀerent behavior from the similar internal shell command. More precisely, cd is implemented in amsh as a function that parses the received path and checks whether this path does not somehow cross a mountpoint deﬁned in a conﬁguration ﬁle similar to /etc/fstab. If so, and if the mountpoint is not installed in the ﬁle system, cd ﬁrst performs the mount operation with the respective ﬁle system and only then changes the directory accordingly. If the mountpoint was already installed, cd follows the usual behavior by changing the directory accordingly. Similarly, any path provided as a parameter to an external command that traverses such a mountpoint must be parsed. If the mountpoint is not installed, it must ﬁrst be included in the ﬁlesystem with the mount command and only then is the shell command that requires the mountpoint executed."},{"t":"In summary, any command that touches such a mountpoint will generate a corresponding mount","x":"In summary, any command that touches such a mountpoint will generate a corresponding mount operation if the respective ﬁle system was not already mounted. Once installed in the ﬁle system, such a mountpoint has a limited lifetime, which can be set in the conﬁguration ﬁle, say 5 minutes. After this period, amsh checks if there are any processes using the mountpoint and, if not, unmounts the mountpoint by calling the umount command. Otherwise, it leaves the mountpoint installed. Note: if the shell itself uses such a mountpoint, the simple operation of changing the directory outside the root subtree in the mountpoint should cause it to be uninstalled at the end of the next 5 min period, provided that no other program has used the mountpoint in the meantime. 10. File&DiskUsageMonitor (2 students) – The script command allows recording a shell session in a ﬁle named implicitly typescript. Write a shell program (script) that uses one or more typescript records to analyze and monitor the evolution over time of the ﬁle and directory structure of interest to the user, as well as the disk space used. Speciﬁcally, the program can analyze the output of ls -l commands at diﬀerent times in time (i.e., between diﬀerent typescript ﬁles, or between a speciﬁc typescript ﬁle and the current moment) and generate a report on the ﬁles/directories that have appeared/disappeared over time. The diﬀerence between the results of diﬀerent ls commands can be done with the diff command, but the program must display these diﬀerences in a form accessible to the non- professional user. Similarly, the program must analyze the disk space usage as reported by the df command. Write a graphical interface (e.g., based on libraries like ncurses and/or utilities like dialog or whiptail) that helps the user vizualize the information in a more convenient way. 11. File&DiskUsageMonitorInotify (2 students) – Write a shell program (script) that im- plements the File&DiskUsageMonitor functionality above without using typescript logging, but the Linux inotify interface to monitor ﬁle and directory changes and display the diﬀe- rences recorded over time, respectively the variation in disk space usage. N.B. Use the commands that implement the functionality of the inotify interface, not the programming API (ie, write a shell program, not a C program). 12. ProxyWebServer (2–3 students) – Write a shell script that uses the wget command to implement a proxy Web server. For this purpose, a local Apache/node.js/nginx, etc. Web server must be set up that serves a local Web page (at the address loopback at the beginning) that allows loading remote Web pages. The page contains at least a form with a ﬁeld where URLs can be entered. As the remote HTML documents are stored locally, the proxy takes over the local caching function and serves the documents already downloaded from the local disk, without generating a request to the Internet. To actually transfer the Web pages to the local system you can use the wget command. On the ﬁrst access of a given URL, when the page is not in the local cache, a situation designated as a cache miss, the proxy creates a local link for the requested HTML document and monitors the local loading of the document through the inotify interface. When the wget command that fetched the document from the remote location has ﬁnished, the proxy serves the client’s request from the local cache. N.B. Depending on how you use the wget command, you can also implement the prefetching functionality, in which you load into the local cache not only the requested document but also"},{"t":"the documents it refers to. Be careful, however, that the procedure can be very expensive ","x":"the documents it refers to. Be careful, however, that the procedure can be very expensive if there are many documents to download (ideally, implement a lazy wget functionality like in one of the previous topics). The local cache is implemented on a temporary ﬁle system tmpfs for performance reasons. At each system boot its content is deleted and the proxy will start with a cold cache. To test this functionality there is no need to reboot the system, it is enough to unmount the temporary ﬁle system with the umount command. The cache implements an LRU (Least Recently Used) policy to remove the oldest document from the cache when a new request no longer has a place in the cache, the cache size being actually the size of the tmpfs system you created (N.B. tmpfs uses swap space, but the requirement is that the cached ﬁles be delivered exclusively from RAM). Test the proxy server using multiple client browsers, run either from the local machine or from the internet if you have the possibility to make the proxy server accessible beyond the local network you are working in (possibly, after you have tested it suﬃciently, you can try uploading it to a web hosting platform; it is best to look for a free web hosting service). 13. Pdsh/pdcp (3 students) – Write shell programs (scripts) that partially implement the func- tionality of the pdsh, pdcp and rdcp commands. As a method for running remote commands, use exclusively ssh, set up appropriately. At a minimum, the script must be able to manipu- late lists of nodes expressed by expressions using the -w and -x ﬂags, as in the examples in the pdsh/pdcp command man pages and as in the examples below: $ mypdsh -w foo[1-5] ”ps -auxw grep %u wc -l” $ mypdcp -w foo[0-5] -x foo[1-3] /etc/hosts /etc $ myrdcp -w foo[7,9-10] /etc/hosts /etc where %u and %h should be replaced with the remote username and remote hostname res- pectively as in the man pages. To test scripts on multiple nodes, use properly conﬁgured virtual machines that can be acces- sed with names like in the commands above (e.g., foo1, foo2, etc.). To build a variable list of nodes, use an automated procedure that duplicates the distribution of a ready-made virtual machine. In other words, ﬁrst manually create a Linux virtual machine that is properly con- ﬁgured to be accessible over the internet and with ssh, then write a shell script duplicateVM that duplicates the previously created virtual machine a number of times speciﬁed as a para- meter in the command line. After duplication, set the hostnames of the newly created virtual machines accordingly so that they can be used as above with the ’pdsh/pdcp type commands. Tip: for performance reasons, use type-1 hypervisors such as xen or kvm and do not create more than 5-10 nodes depending on the conﬁguration of the virtual machines (disk space, RAM, number of virtual processors). 14. NestedVMs∗(3 students) – Create a distributed virtual system on the local machine built from virtual machines that run inside other virtual machines (nested VMs). Speciﬁcally, on the host machine you install a virtual machine that you then automatically duplicate with a script in a variable number of new VM instances. Then repeat the procedure in each of the newly created virtual machines, which thus become host machines for the guest virtual machines that they create inside them. The net result is a hierarchy of virtual machines created recursively inside each other, like in a tree in which the root node is the host machine"},{"t":"(the physical machine you are using), and at each level in the tree the nodes represent gu","x":"(the physical machine you are using), and at each level in the tree the nodes represent guest virtual machines that in turn are roots for sub-trees of virtual machines created inside these virtual machines. For example, for testing you can use the following structure: host machine – guest VM1 – guest VM1-1 – guest VM1-2 – guest VM2 – guest VM2-1 – guest VM2-2 Set up the virtual machines in your distributed virtual system component to be able to route network packets between them, regardless of the nesting level they are in. For example, using the hierarchy above, from machine VM2-2 you should be able to execute commands like: $ ping VM1-2 $ ping VM1 Restrict incoming traﬃc to virtual machines to ICMP traﬃc (to be able to execute the ping command) and ports 22 and 80 to be able to run the sshd and httpd daemons and thus allow remote access to virtual machines and the installation of Web servers. Outgoing traﬃc (from inside the virtual machine to the outside) can remain unrestricted. Install a preferred web server (Apache,node.js,nginx, etc.) on the host machine and con- ﬁgure it accordingly. Then install a web server on each virtual machine in the hierarchy (possibly automatically, using a script) and use a scatter-like command to set automatically the Web server conﬁgurations on each of the created virtual machines by copying the server conﬁguration ﬁles from the host machine. The scatter command should be able to be run as follows from the root of any sub-tree in the created virtual machine hierarchy, as below: $ scatter httpd.conf /etc Run on the host machine, for example, the above command has the eﬀect of copying the server conﬁguration ﬁle (located in the current directory) to all /etc directories of the virtual machines in the created hierarchy. Similarly, implement the gather command that gathers on the node on which it is running all copies of the ﬁle speciﬁed in the command line from the virtual machines in the subtree whose root is the node on which the command is running, as below: $ gather /etc/httpd.conf /etc Run on the host machine, for example, the above command collects in the local /etc directory all copies of the Web server conﬁguration ﬁles from the virtual machines in the created hierarchy (possibly extended with a suﬃx representing the name of the machine they come from, for example httpd.conf.VM2-1). Conﬁgure this collection of Web servers to work together, such that HTML documents at the deepest level of virtual machine nesting are available through any server above them in the hierarchy. At the host machine level, the local Web server becomes the gateway to a distributed Web server."},{"t":"Tip : for performance reasons, use type-1 hypervisors such as xen or kvm and make sure tha","x":"Tip: for performance reasons, use type-1 hypervisors such as xen or kvm and make sure that the host machine’s processor supports SLAT (Second Level Address Translation) and HW virtualization technologies (eg, run the kvm-ok command to ﬁnd out if you can run the project on your personal computer)."}]}];
